{"parentUuid":"722c2b89-f018-4ac6-993e-52b5de0baa27","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EReuq5mSnweNVpFbBCS2qm","type":"tool_result","content":"15\t| Equation 4.1 placement and impulse \"where s is the displacement\", PDF 257 / p.246 | Both oddities recorded correctly in `claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant`. But that entry has a separate physics error (see its FIX row). |\n16\t| 4.3.3 effort rule (\"not less ... even exceed\" vs \"moderate amount of effort\"), PDF 256-257 / pp.245-246 | Handled honestly in `principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed` (both quoted, reconciliation labelled Inference). |\n17\t| Aerobic/anaerobic antagonism offered p.249, questioned p.250 | Handled honestly in `claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance` (Why it works keeps both) and `claim--local-blood-flow-...` (When NOT). |\n18\t| Pattengale & Holloszy 1967 | Handled honestly in `claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport`. Reviewer confirmed independently: PubMed 6036801, \"Augmentation of skeletal muscle myoglobin by a program of treadmill running,\" Am J Physiol 1967;213(3):783-5. The entry's citation details are correct. |\n19\t| Table column sums | Table 4.1 sums checked: 29.6+19.6+13.0+10.5+6.5 = 79.2; 27.4+23.4+18.5+11.8 = 81.1 (entry says about 79% and 81%: correct). Table 4.2: 35.9+23.3+16.8+2.7+8.9 = 87.6; 36.5+21.9+19.8+10.9 = 89.1 (entry says about 88% and 89%: correct). Not flagged by the writer: Table 4.2 Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against factor ordering (see skater FIX row). |\n20\t\n21\t## Entry verdicts\n22\t\n23\t| File | Verdict | Problem (entry quote / source quote with page) | Exact correction |\n24\t|---|---|---|---|\n25\t| framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md | FIX | When NOT, Inference: \"when the criteria conflict (for example, matching amplitude may require an awkward position that breaks the regime of work, as the authors' own Fig 4.22 discussion shows, PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245), the text gives no ranking among them.\" The source says the opposite: \"Following these requirements can sometimes lead to such initial positions ... which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22). However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). The authors deny a conflict; nothing there mentions the regime of work. Rest checked: five criteria list at PDF 252 / p.241; \"All sporting movement is specific and goal-directed\", \"One should not refer to strength in general\", \"an adequate conditioning regime\" at PDF 252; \"success in selecting the means ... biomechanics of the movements\" at PDF 259 / p.248; \"only part of the process\" at PDF 266 / p.255 (opened); baseline line 103 quote exact. | Replace that sentence with: \"The text gives no ranking among the criteria. The authors say awkward positions such as Fig 4.22 arise \"only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245), so they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\" |\n26\t| heuristic--match-amplitude-direction-and-line-of-resistance-to-the-sport.md | GO | checked: rower vs shot putter, shoulder extension vs flexion (Fig 4.17) at PDF 253 / p.242; \"begins at an angle of about 210° in the hip joint, relative to the torso\" at PDF 253; Fig 4.18 description against image (inclined bench, leg swinging up through an arc) at PDF 253; knapsack/weight belt quotes at PDF 253; skater push-off \"where the force is directed backward\" at PDF 253; Fig 4.19 (tether, weighted sled, partner on sled) against image at PDF 254 / p.243; \"select the exact starting position ...\" at PDF 253; baseline line 103; sled inference labelled. | None. |\n27\t| heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation.md | GO | checked: \"simultaneous flexion and extension at the two hip joints ...\" and \"usually inappropriate to perform the actual sporting movements with significantly large added loads ...\" at PDF 254 / p.243; Fig 4.20 against image: eight drawings (two on a vaulting horse, two at wall bars, two on a box with a partner, two hanging from rings), no loads shown; caption exact; football example labelled Inference; baseline line 103. | None. |\n28\t| heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md | FIX | Why it works: \"drag in fluid flow scaling with velocity squared is standard fluid mechanics.\" The author says only \"(actually the square of the velocity)\" (PDF 255 / p.244); \"standard fluid mechanics\" is the writer's uncited judgment, the \"well established\" phrasing the brief bans. Rest checked: Popov 1966, Brunner & Tabachnik 1990, \"more than 20 years earlier\" at PDF 254 / p.243; \"5 - 200 Newtons for running speeds of 6 -10 metres per second ... (Zatsiorsky, 1995)\" at PDF 255 / p.244 (image); size-to-quality mapping, 30-150 m, progression, bracketing with unresisted runs, two or three times a week, later preparatory phase, all at PDF 255; \"plays no role in the development of starting strength\" at PDF 254; baseline line 97 quote exact. | Replace the first sentence of Why it works with: \"The authors give the physics: drag \"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity),\" which is why parachute size and running speed together set the load. Inference: velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`; ...\" (keep the rest of that paragraph). |\n29\t| claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md | FIX | When to apply: \"if vertical loading is the concern (heavy athletes, athletes returning from lower-limb injury), the authors' argument favours the parachute\". The book names no populations; it says only \"they do not increase the vertical reaction forces acting on the body\" (PDF 255 / p.244). Unlabelled addition. Rest checked: safer-alternative quote across PDF 254-255; support and non-support phases quote, landing joint positions \"especially during hurdling and jumping\", \"All of this can adversely affect ...\" at PDF 255 / p.244; starting-strength sentence at PDF 254; baseline line 103. | Change to: \"if vertical loading is the concern, the authors' argument favours the parachute (Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population); if preserving flight-phase and landing mechanics is the concern (the book names hurdlers and jumpers), it counts against it.\" |\n30\t| heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | FIX | \"The authors also show a partner exercise (Fig 4.22 ...) where trying to meet the criteria gives \"incongruous and artificial\" positions.\" This drops the next sentence of the source, which reverses the meaning: \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). Rest checked: \"Muscular effort changes ...\", ballistic vs mixed-regime instant (Verkhoshansky 1977), \"accentuated region ... specific joint angle\" at PDF 255 / p.244; Fig 4.21 values against image (see oddities table); \"lose the ability to train the muscles ...\" and \"selection of these exercises should be based on the joint angle at which maximal torque is developed\" at PDF 256 / p.245; Fig 4.22 against image (partner on shoulders, partner pulling back leg, wall bars); 15-degree cross-reference matches the vault 4.2.4 entry; baseline line 78. | Replace with: \"The authors say that following these requirements \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22),\" but add: \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245). Fig 4.22 (verified against the page image: ...)\" then keep the caption and \"applied carefully\" sentences. |\n31\t| principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed.md | GO | checked: criterion definition, \"should not be less than ... and should even exceed it\", maximum vs mean question, \"F = ma\", \"absolute strength\" vs \"speed of contraction\", \"can be determined only by calculating the speed of movement\" at PDF 256 / p.245; heavy/slow and light/fast examples at PDF 257 / p.246 (image); effort ambiguity kept and reconciliation labelled Inference; baseline line 104 quote exact; line 76 quote exact and its \"[contested]\" tag confirmed in the baseline text. | None. |\n32\t| claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md | FIX | Physics error carried into a `proven` entry. Title: \"P = F x V Holds Only When Force Is Constant\". Why it works: \"using it with a time-varying force gives a wrong instantaneous picture.\" When NOT: \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" Instantaneous power P(t) = F(t)·v(t) holds for any force history (dW = F·ds, so dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v). Constant force is needed only to take F outside the integral, that is, to write power over an interval as one force times the average velocity. The book's statement (\"if F is constant over the interval concerned, we obtain ... P = F.V\", \"If the force is not constant ... we have to rely on a more precise form such as equation (4.1)\", PDF 257 / p.246, image) is imprecise, and the entry adopts it as `proven`. The authors' valid practical point is that average (or peak) force times average (or peak) velocity is not average (or peak) power, and \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone.\" Quotes otherwise checked at PDF 257: P = dW/dt, W = ∫F(t).ds, italic sentence, plyometric \"transient spike function\", force plate or high speed video; Eq 4.1 and impulse oddities correctly described; baseline line 79. | (1) Retitle: \"The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve\". Slug may stay; if renamed, update the one Related link in `heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md`. (2) Replace the `proven` scope sentence in What it is and Why it works with: \"Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force. The authors state that P = F.V needs constant force; that is true only when one force value stands for the whole interval (power over the interval = F times average velocity). Inference: for a time-varying force, the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact; what fails is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity, which is the misuse the authors' warning actually targets.\" (3) Delete \"so using it with a time-varying force gives a wrong instantaneous picture\". (4) In When NOT replace \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" with \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\" |\n33\t| heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md | GO | checked: criterion \"complements the criterion of dynamic effort ... explosive strength\" and \"The ability to increase power or explosive strength also depends on specifically training the RFD factor\", \"vital\", plyometrics as \"practical consequences\" at PDF 257 / p.246; RC = Fmax/tmax.mg = RFDmax/W against image; impulse \"acceptable only when it is not necessary to compare different movements ... irrelevant\" at PDF 257; \"he must do it quickly and use a high RFD\", \"especially the working amplitude\", \"all training is primarily neuromuscular ... highly inadequate\" at PDF 258 / p.247; baseline lines 74 and 77 (tags exact). | None (if the power entry is renamed, update its link here). |\n34\t| heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md | FIX | When to apply: \"single maximal actions against a fixed implement call for heavier loads, few repetitions and full recovery.\" The book says only \"rapid single movements against a specific resistance\" and \"different weights, tempos, number of sets and repetitions\" (PDF 258 / p.247); \"full recovery\" is not in the source. Rest checked: criterion wording, \"the same means can solve different tasks\", boxer/shot putter quote, two tasks, \"still far from being resolved\" at PDF 258; baseline line 102 quote and tag exact. | Change to: \"single fast actions against a set resistance call for heavier loads and few repetitions (Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest).\" |\n35\t| claim--regime-switching-ability-decides-mixed-regime-events.md | GO | checked: \"changing from one activity to another\", long jump \"determined mainly by the ability to switch ...\", gymnasts \"dynamic explosive effort to isometric tension\", \"aim of technical training ... goal of special strength training\" at PDF 258 / p.247; mixed-regime force peak \"at the instant of switching\" at PDF 255 / p.244; link between them labelled Inference; football example labelled; `untested` justified (no data). | None. |\n36\t| claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic.md | GO | checked: \"decathlon, gymnastics and modem pentathlon\"; \"Almost one hundred years ... speed-cyclic with dynamic tension, primarily of the explosive type\"; Zatsiorsky 1966, Lukauskas 1967, Mamdzhanyan 1976; gymnasts and \"extensive longitudinal rotation\"; \"cyclic and phasic-tonic regimes probably ...\"; \"needs rigorous experimental substantiation\", all at PDF 258 / p.247; When to apply opens with Inference; baseline line 182. `untested` defensible on the authors' own caveat. | None. |\n37\t| heuristic--phasic-biomechanical-analysis-before-selecting-special-strength-means.md | GO | checked: high speed video quote PDF 258-259; \"fundamental or supportive role\", \"obtained only from computerised video analysis\", Donskoi phasic structure, force plate or cable tensiometer, \"exercise locally the interacting muscle groups\", heterochronicity and slower development of indirectly interacting elements, closing sentence, all at PDF 259 / p.248; Example labelled Inference; baseline line 33 quote exact. Minor: Related labels the heterochronicity entry \"Ch2.2.4\" while the text cross-references 2.2.3; the linked entry's own chapter field is 2.2.4, so the label is correct. | None. |\n38\t| claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance.md | GO | checked: \"solely at the level of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems is inadequate\" at PDF 259 / p.248; Faulkner 1968 1940s runners; Nabotnikova 1972, Serafimova 1974, Rusko 1976; Orlov & Sharova 1977 skaters 4-5 years; Melenberg et al 1972 cyclists; competition-period VO2 max decrease list; R = 0.7 preparation vs 0.4 competition at PDF 260 / p.249 (image); \"no guarantee of outstanding performance\"; both antagonism statements kept (p.249 offered, p.250 questioned); baseline line 113. | None. |\n39\t| claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max.md | GO | checked against image PDF 260 / p.249: Ekblom 1969, Rowell 1971, Saltin 1974; four citations for \"decrease the rate of glycolysis\" (Newshalme & Randle, Saltin & Karlsson, Paul et al, Grimby et al); Jorfeldt 1970, Knutten 1971, Gollnick & Hermansen 1973; italic conclusion; \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\"; \"eliminating the disparity\"; contested reasons: raise VO2 max (PDF 260) vs \"does not lie in raising VO2 max\" (PDF 261 / p.250), high blood lactate part of load (PDF 260) and skaters' glycolytic productivity (PDF 264 / p.253) vs less glycolysis; Christensen & Hogberg 50% sentence (\"include anaerobic energy production\" confirmed on image); baseline lines 124, 116, 117. `contested` has a stated reason. | None. |\n40\t| claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains.md | GO | checked: nine haemodynamic citations counted on PDF 260 image (Vasiliev 1970 and 1974, Ozolin & Partsik, Danilov, Kurbanov, Rowell, Kaijser, Karlsson, Gollnick); italic redistribution sentence; Stepochkin et al 1970 25 km cyclists, VO2 max and cardiac output down, leg blood flow up, at PDF 260 / p.249; OBLA rises while VO2 max falls; skaters VO2 max 5-10% vs local blood flow 50-250% (Mellenberg 1981) at PDF 261 / p.250 (image); antagonism \"must be questioned (Mellenberg & Khvan, 1982)\" across PDF 260-261; \"vesicular\" gloss labelled Inference; 6%/7% handled; baseline line 123. | None. |\n41\t| principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles.md | GO | checked at PDF 261 / p.250 (image): \"adaptation of the muscles to anaerobic work is clearly local in nature\"; \"approximately equivalent functional effect ... only ... while using those same muscle groups (Clausen et al, 1970; Holmer & Astrand, 1972)\"; skier example and Yevstratov et al 1975; local muscular endurance definition; \"still need serious research\"; Verkhoshansky 1977 prescription; baseline line 122 quote exact. | None. |\n42\t| claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance.md | GO | checked at PDF 261 / p.250: both quoted sentences exact; Fig 4.23 against image: x-axis 53 to 46 s, left axes stride length 200-230 cm and stride rate 3.7-3.9 per s, right axis number of strides 170-200; one line rises from about 207 to about 227 cm, steep after about 48 s (labelled stride length); a line labelled stride rate peaks near 50 s and falls toward 46 s. Entry's reading and its caution about the other two lines are accurate. The cross-sectional vs within-race point is labelled Inference and is a fair falsification of the figure's support for the fatigue claim. Baseline line 96. | None. |\n43\t| claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport.md | GO | checked: full citation sentence across PDF 261-262 (Petrov & Lapchenkov 1978; Pattengale & Holloszy 1967; Hemmingsen 1963; Neminushchii & Filin 1972; Gavrilenko & Mikhailov 1981); seven local-endurance citations 1962-1974 counted at PDF 262 / p.251; \"it has not been given serious attention\"; Pattengale & Holloszy title, journal, volume and pages confirmed by reviewer (PubMed 6036801); `contested` reason stated; Example labelled Inference; baseline line 182. | None. |\n44\t| principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them.md | GO | checked at PDF 262 / p.251 (image): six citations for \"complex resultant of motor abilities\" (Reindel, Roskamm, Brogli & Antonov, Larson & Yocom, Posker & Steblo, Wilt); general endurance definition; \"training complexes\"; Mogendovich 1962 and Arshavsky 1967; efficiency sentence; motor processes \"play the key role\"; \"a serious error to draw a line between them\"; Example labelled Inference; baseline line 122. | None. |"}]},"uuid":"5040e9ca-3eee-42cd-aaec-ed5b3eb18b47","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:06.721Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md","content":"| Equation 4.1 placement and impulse \"where s is the displacement\", PDF 257 / p.246 | Both oddities recorded correctly in `claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant`. But that entry has a separate physics error (see its FIX row). |\n| 4.3.3 effort rule (\"not less ... even exceed\" vs \"moderate amount of effort\"), PDF 256-257 / pp.245-246 | Handled honestly in `principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed` (both quoted, reconciliation labelled Inference). |\n| Aerobic/anaerobic antagonism offered p.249, questioned p.250 | Handled honestly in `claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance` (Why it works keeps both) and `claim--local-blood-flow-...` (When NOT). |\n| Pattengale & Holloszy 1967 | Handled honestly in `claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport`. Reviewer confirmed independently: PubMed 6036801, \"Augmentation of skeletal muscle myoglobin by a program of treadmill running,\" Am J Physiol 1967;213(3):783-5. The entry's citation details are correct. |\n| Table column sums | Table 4.1 sums checked: 29.6+19.6+13.0+10.5+6.5 = 79.2; 27.4+23.4+18.5+11.8 = 81.1 (entry says about 79% and 81%: correct). Table 4.2: 35.9+23.3+16.8+2.7+8.9 = 87.6; 36.5+21.9+19.8+10.9 = 89.1 (entry says about 88% and 89%: correct). Not flagged by the writer: Table 4.2 Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against factor ordering (see skater FIX row). |\n\n## Entry verdicts\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry quote / source quote with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md | FIX | When NOT, Inference: \"when the criteria conflict (for example, matching amplitude may require an awkward position that breaks the regime of work, as the authors' own Fig 4.22 discussion shows, PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245), the text gives no ranking among them.\" The source says the opposite: \"Following these requirements can sometimes lead to such initial positions ... which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22). However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). The authors deny a conflict; nothing there mentions the regime of work. Rest checked: five criteria list at PDF 252 / p.241; \"All sporting movement is specific and goal-directed\", \"One should not refer to strength in general\", \"an adequate conditioning regime\" at PDF 252; \"success in selecting the means ... biomechanics of the movements\" at PDF 259 / p.248; \"only part of the process\" at PDF 266 / p.255 (opened); baseline line 103 quote exact. | Replace that sentence with: \"The text gives no ranking among the criteria. The authors say awkward positions such as Fig 4.22 arise \"only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245), so they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\" |\n| heuristic--match-amplitude-direction-and-line-of-resistance-to-the-sport.md | GO | checked: rower vs shot putter, shoulder extension vs flexion (Fig 4.17) at PDF 253 / p.242; \"begins at an angle of about 210° in the hip joint, relative to the torso\" at PDF 253; Fig 4.18 description against image (inclined bench, leg swinging up through an arc) at PDF 253; knapsack/weight belt quotes at PDF 253; skater push-off \"where the force is directed backward\" at PDF 253; Fig 4.19 (tether, weighted sled, partner on sled) against image at PDF 254 / p.243; \"select the exact starting position ...\" at PDF 253; baseline line 103; sled inference labelled. | None. |\n| heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation.md | GO | checked: \"simultaneous flexion and extension at the two hip joints ...\" and \"usually inappropriate to perform the actual sporting movements with significantly large added loads ...\" at PDF 254 / p.243; Fig 4.20 against image: eight drawings (two on a vaulting horse, two at wall bars, two on a box with a partner, two hanging from rings), no loads shown; caption exact; football example labelled Inference; baseline line 103. | None. |\n| heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md | FIX | Why it works: \"drag in fluid flow scaling with velocity squared is standard fluid mechanics.\" The author says only \"(actually the square of the velocity)\" (PDF 255 / p.244); \"standard fluid mechanics\" is the writer's uncited judgment, the \"well established\" phrasing the brief bans. Rest checked: Popov 1966, Brunner & Tabachnik 1990, \"more than 20 years earlier\" at PDF 254 / p.243; \"5 - 200 Newtons for running speeds of 6 -10 metres per second ... (Zatsiorsky, 1995)\" at PDF 255 / p.244 (image); size-to-quality mapping, 30-150 m, progression, bracketing with unresisted runs, two or three times a week, later preparatory phase, all at PDF 255; \"plays no role in the development of starting strength\" at PDF 254; baseline line 97 quote exact. | Replace the first sentence of Why it works with: \"The authors give the physics: drag \"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity),\" which is why parachute size and running speed together set the load. Inference: velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`; ...\" (keep the rest of that paragraph). |\n| claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md | FIX | When to apply: \"if vertical loading is the concern (heavy athletes, athletes returning from lower-limb injury), the authors' argument favours the parachute\". The book names no populations; it says only \"they do not increase the vertical reaction forces acting on the body\" (PDF 255 / p.244). Unlabelled addition. Rest checked: safer-alternative quote across PDF 254-255; support and non-support phases quote, landing joint positions \"especially during hurdling and jumping\", \"All of this can adversely affect ...\" at PDF 255 / p.244; starting-strength sentence at PDF 254; baseline line 103. | Change to: \"if vertical loading is the concern, the authors' argument favours the parachute (Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population); if preserving flight-phase and landing mechanics is the concern (the book names hurdlers and jumpers), it counts against it.\" |\n| heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | FIX | \"The authors also show a partner exercise (Fig 4.22 ...) where trying to meet the criteria gives \"incongruous and artificial\" positions.\" This drops the next sentence of the source, which reverses the meaning: \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). Rest checked: \"Muscular effort changes ...\", ballistic vs mixed-regime instant (Verkhoshansky 1977), \"accentuated region ... specific joint angle\" at PDF 255 / p.244; Fig 4.21 values against image (see oddities table); \"lose the ability to train the muscles ...\" and \"selection of these exercises should be based on the joint angle at which maximal torque is developed\" at PDF 256 / p.245; Fig 4.22 against image (partner on shoulders, partner pulling back leg, wall bars); 15-degree cross-reference matches the vault 4.2.4 entry; baseline line 78. | Replace with: \"The authors say that following these requirements \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22),\" but add: \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245). Fig 4.22 (verified against the page image: ...)\" then keep the caption and \"applied carefully\" sentences. |\n| principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed.md | GO | checked: criterion definition, \"should not be less than ... and should even exceed it\", maximum vs mean question, \"F = ma\", \"absolute strength\" vs \"speed of contraction\", \"can be determined only by calculating the speed of movement\" at PDF 256 / p.245; heavy/slow and light/fast examples at PDF 257 / p.246 (image); effort ambiguity kept and reconciliation labelled Inference; baseline line 104 quote exact; line 76 quote exact and its \"[contested]\" tag confirmed in the baseline text. | None. |\n| claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md | FIX | Physics error carried into a `proven` entry. Title: \"P = F x V Holds Only When Force Is Constant\". Why it works: \"using it with a time-varying force gives a wrong instantaneous picture.\" When NOT: \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" Instantaneous power P(t) = F(t)·v(t) holds for any force history (dW = F·ds, so dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v). Constant force is needed only to take F outside the integral, that is, to write power over an interval as one force times the average velocity. The book's statement (\"if F is constant over the interval concerned, we obtain ... P = F.V\", \"If the force is not constant ... we have to rely on a more precise form such as equation (4.1)\", PDF 257 / p.246, image) is imprecise, and the entry adopts it as `proven`. The authors' valid practical point is that average (or peak) force times average (or peak) velocity is not average (or peak) power, and \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone.\" Quotes otherwise checked at PDF 257: P = dW/dt, W = ∫F(t).ds, italic sentence, plyometric \"transient spike function\", force plate or high speed video; Eq 4.1 and impulse oddities correctly described; baseline line 79. | (1) Retitle: \"The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve\". Slug may stay; if renamed, update the one Related link in `heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md`. (2) Replace the `proven` scope sentence in What it is and Why it works with: \"Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force. The authors state that P = F.V needs constant force; that is true only when one force value stands for the whole interval (power over the interval = F times average velocity). Inference: for a time-varying force, the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact; what fails is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity, which is the misuse the authors' warning actually targets.\" (3) Delete \"so using it with a time-varying force gives a wrong instantaneous picture\". (4) In When NOT replace \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" with \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\" |\n| heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md | GO | checked: criterion \"complements the criterion of dynamic effort ... explosive strength\" and \"The ability to increase power or explosive strength also depends on specifically training the RFD factor\", \"vital\", plyometrics as \"practical consequences\" at PDF 257 / p.246; RC = Fmax/tmax.mg = RFDmax/W against image; impulse \"acceptable only when it is not necessary to compare different movements ... irrelevant\" at PDF 257; \"he must do it quickly and use a high RFD\", \"especially the working amplitude\", \"all training is primarily neuromuscular ... highly inadequate\" at PDF 258 / p.247; baseline lines 74 and 77 (tags exact). | None (if the power entry is renamed, update its link here). |\n| heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md | FIX | When to apply: \"single maximal actions against a fixed implement call for heavier loads, few repetitions and full recovery.\" The book says only \"rapid single movements against a specific resistance\" and \"different weights, tempos, number of sets and repetitions\" (PDF 258 / p.247); \"full recovery\" is not in the source. Rest checked: criterion wording, \"the same means can solve different tasks\", boxer/shot putter quote, two tasks, \"still far from being resolved\" at PDF 258; baseline line 102 quote and tag exact. | Change to: \"single fast actions against a set resistance call for heavier loads and few repetitions (Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest).\" |\n| claim--regime-switching-ability-decides-mixed-regime-events.md | GO | checked: \"changing from one activity to another\", long jump \"determined mainly by the ability to switch ...\", gymnasts \"dynamic explosive effort to isometric tension\", \"aim of technical training ... goal of special strength training\" at PDF 258 / p.247; mixed-regime force peak \"at the instant of switching\" at PDF 255 / p.244; link between them labelled Inference; football example labelled; `untested` justified (no data). | None. |\n| claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic.md | GO | checked: \"decathlon, gymnastics and modem pentathlon\"; \"Almost one hundred years ... speed-cyclic with dynamic tension, primarily of the explosive type\"; Zatsiorsky 1966, Lukauskas 1967, Mamdzhanyan 1976; gymnasts and \"extensive longitudinal rotation\"; \"cyclic and phasic-tonic regimes probably ...\"; \"needs rigorous experimental substantiation\", all at PDF 258 / p.247; When to apply opens with Inference; baseline line 182. `untested` defensible on the authors' own caveat. | None. |\n| heuristic--phasic-biomechanical-analysis-before-selecting-special-strength-means.md | GO | checked: high speed video quote PDF 258-259; \"fundamental or supportive role\", \"obtained only from computerised video analysis\", Donskoi phasic structure, force plate or cable tensiometer, \"exercise locally the interacting muscle groups\", heterochronicity and slower development of indirectly interacting elements, closing sentence, all at PDF 259 / p.248; Example labelled Inference; baseline line 33 quote exact. Minor: Related labels the heterochronicity entry \"Ch2.2.4\" while the text cross-references 2.2.3; the linked entry's own chapter field is 2.2.4, so the label is correct. | None. |\n| claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance.md | GO | checked: \"solely at the level of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems is inadequate\" at PDF 259 / p.248; Faulkner 1968 1940s runners; Nabotnikova 1972, Serafimova 1974, Rusko 1976; Orlov & Sharova 1977 skaters 4-5 years; Melenberg et al 1972 cyclists; competition-period VO2 max decrease list; R = 0.7 preparation vs 0.4 competition at PDF 260 / p.249 (image); \"no guarantee of outstanding performance\"; both antagonism statements kept (p.249 offered, p.250 questioned); baseline line 113. | None. |\n| claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max.md | GO | checked against image PDF 260 / p.249: Ekblom 1969, Rowell 1971, Saltin 1974; four citations for \"decrease the rate of glycolysis\" (Newshalme & Randle, Saltin & Karlsson, Paul et al, Grimby et al); Jorfeldt 1970, Knutten 1971, Gollnick & Hermansen 1973; italic conclusion; \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\"; \"eliminating the disparity\"; contested reasons: raise VO2 max (PDF 260) vs \"does not lie in raising VO2 max\" (PDF 261 / p.250), high blood lactate part of load (PDF 260) and skaters' glycolytic productivity (PDF 264 / p.253) vs less glycolysis; Christensen & Hogberg 50% sentence (\"include anaerobic energy production\" confirmed on image); baseline lines 124, 116, 117. `contested` has a stated reason. | None. |\n| claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains.md | GO | checked: nine haemodynamic citations counted on PDF 260 image (Vasiliev 1970 and 1974, Ozolin & Partsik, Danilov, Kurbanov, Rowell, Kaijser, Karlsson, Gollnick); italic redistribution sentence; Stepochkin et al 1970 25 km cyclists, VO2 max and cardiac output down, leg blood flow up, at PDF 260 / p.249; OBLA rises while VO2 max falls; skaters VO2 max 5-10% vs local blood flow 50-250% (Mellenberg 1981) at PDF 261 / p.250 (image); antagonism \"must be questioned (Mellenberg & Khvan, 1982)\" across PDF 260-261; \"vesicular\" gloss labelled Inference; 6%/7% handled; baseline line 123. | None. |\n| principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles.md | GO | checked at PDF 261 / p.250 (image): \"adaptation of the muscles to anaerobic work is clearly local in nature\"; \"approximately equivalent functional effect ... only ... while using those same muscle groups (Clausen et al, 1970; Holmer & Astrand, 1972)\"; skier example and Yevstratov et al 1975; local muscular endurance definition; \"still need serious research\"; Verkhoshansky 1977 prescription; baseline line 122 quote exact. | None. |\n| claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance.md | GO | checked at PDF 261 / p.250: both quoted sentences exact; Fig 4.23 against image: x-axis 53 to 46 s, left axes stride length 200-230 cm and stride rate 3.7-3.9 per s, right axis number of strides 170-200; one line rises from about 207 to about 227 cm, steep after about 48 s (labelled stride length); a line labelled stride rate peaks near 50 s and falls toward 46 s. Entry's reading and its caution about the other two lines are accurate. The cross-sectional vs within-race point is labelled Inference and is a fair falsification of the figure's support for the fatigue claim. Baseline line 96. | None. |\n| claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport.md | GO | checked: full citation sentence across PDF 261-262 (Petrov & Lapchenkov 1978; Pattengale & Holloszy 1967; Hemmingsen 1963; Neminushchii & Filin 1972; Gavrilenko & Mikhailov 1981); seven local-endurance citations 1962-1974 counted at PDF 262 / p.251; \"it has not been given serious attention\"; Pattengale & Holloszy title, journal, volume and pages confirmed by reviewer (PubMed 6036801); `contested` reason stated; Example labelled Inference; baseline line 182. | None. |\n| principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them.md | GO | checked at PDF 262 / p.251 (image): six citations for \"complex resultant of motor abilities\" (Reindel, Roskamm, Brogli & Antonov, Larson & Yocom, Posker & Steblo, Wilt); general endurance definition; \"training complexes\"; Mogendovich 1962 and Arshavsky 1967; efficiency sentence; motor processes \"play the key role\"; \"a serious error to draw a line between them\"; Example labelled Inference; baseline line 122. | None. |","numLines":30,"startLine":15,"totalLines":81}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"722c2b89-f018-4ac6-993e-52b5de0baa27","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
