{"parentUuid":"34c7b532-7299-47ea-ae69-44a8c67ee36d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVE2FYYUGAXRbFD2rs8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WD2yv3PYKsT6yA5ABhSemH","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md","offset":45,"limit":5},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9330,"cache_read_input_tokens":59218,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9330,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WD2yv3PYKsT6yA5ABhSemH":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md","offset":45,"limit":5}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffVE1zRBpTbUgjx5KpGu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5cf477a7-55ed-4a4c-85c3-84886c9fc2e7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:09.279Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"5cf477a7-55ed-4a4c-85c3-84886c9fc2e7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WD2yv3PYKsT6yA5ABhSemH","type":"tool_result","content":"45\t| framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness.md | GO | checked Fig 4.24 against image at PDF 262 / p.251: Level 1 speed, strength, endurance (generalised abilities); Level 2 coordination; Level 3 ability to display explosive force and specific endurance; Level 4 structure of special physical preparedness (specialised abilities); Level 1-3 text quotes; specific endurance \"cardiovascular or local muscular\"; cooperation vs subordination and cross-training sentence at PDF 263 / p.252; baseline line 101 quote and tag exact; Vermeil comparison labelled Inference. Note for the fixer (not required): \"build ... first, then ...\" turns the authors' descriptive sequence into a plan; acceptable under the threshold above. | None. |\n46\t| case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | FIX | When to apply: \"as qualification rises, give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position and less to plantarflexor power.\" The source supports the first half (\"the contribution of isometric strength ... assumes great significance for highly qualified athletes\", PDF 263 / p.252) but not \"less to plantarflexor power\": the book says the plantarflexors' share of the factor structure \"diminishes significantly\" and that they \"play a diminishing role\" in take-off dynamics, which describes variance explained, not a training reduction. Rest checked: all nine Table 4.1 values and labels against image at PDF 263 / p.252; sums 79.2 and 81.1; the authors' reading of factors I-V; the lever mechanism and the plantarflexor sentence; \"general trends of change ... individual peculiarities\" at PDF 264 / p.253; baseline line 76. | Change to: \"as qualification rises, give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position. Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction.\" |\n47\t| case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | FIX | Table 4.2 anomaly not flagged, and an Inference built on it. Image (PDF 264 / p.253) confirms Class 1 factor IV \"Energetic efficiency of executing specific work 2.7\" and factor V \"Multi-faceted technical preparedness 8.9\": factor IV is smaller than factor V, against the ordering of factors by share. 12.7 would restore the order and bring the column to 97.6%; the printed value may be a misprint, but the source does not say. The entry's \"the skaters' jump in \"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\" (2.7% to 36.5%)\" relies on that value. Rest checked: all nine values and labels against image; sums 87.6 and 89.1; \"sharp rise\", \"combined as one factor (i.e. aerobic efficiency)\", \"anaerobic power increases slightly (factor III)\" across PDF 263-264; conclusion quote; glycolysis conflict cross-referenced correctly; baseline line 172. | (1) In When NOT add: \"Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against the order of factors by share (verified against the page image); 2.7 may be a misprint (12.7 would restore the order and bring the column to about 98%), but the source gives no correction, so treat the 2.7 value as unclear.\" (2) In When to apply replace the Inference with: \"Inference: the rise of \"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\" to the first factor at Master of Sport level (36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point; the Class 1 value for the same factor (2.7) is unclear, so the size of the change should not be quoted.\" |\n48\t\n49\t## `_book.md` (4.3 and 4.4 rows, newest batch note)"}]},"uuid":"1833110b-5a55-4263-bc45-087e3395e560","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:09.469Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md","content":"| framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness.md | GO | checked Fig 4.24 against image at PDF 262 / p.251: Level 1 speed, strength, endurance (generalised abilities); Level 2 coordination; Level 3 ability to display explosive force and specific endurance; Level 4 structure of special physical preparedness (specialised abilities); Level 1-3 text quotes; specific endurance \"cardiovascular or local muscular\"; cooperation vs subordination and cross-training sentence at PDF 263 / p.252; baseline line 101 quote and tag exact; Vermeil comparison labelled Inference. Note for the fixer (not required): \"build ... first, then ...\" turns the authors' descriptive sequence into a plan; acceptable under the threshold above. | None. |\n| case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | FIX | When to apply: \"as qualification rises, give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position and less to plantarflexor power.\" The source supports the first half (\"the contribution of isometric strength ... assumes great significance for highly qualified athletes\", PDF 263 / p.252) but not \"less to plantarflexor power\": the book says the plantarflexors' share of the factor structure \"diminishes significantly\" and that they \"play a diminishing role\" in take-off dynamics, which describes variance explained, not a training reduction. Rest checked: all nine Table 4.1 values and labels against image at PDF 263 / p.252; sums 79.2 and 81.1; the authors' reading of factors I-V; the lever mechanism and the plantarflexor sentence; \"general trends of change ... individual peculiarities\" at PDF 264 / p.253; baseline line 76. | Change to: \"as qualification rises, give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position. Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction.\" |\n| case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | FIX | Table 4.2 anomaly not flagged, and an Inference built on it. Image (PDF 264 / p.253) confirms Class 1 factor IV \"Energetic efficiency of executing specific work 2.7\" and factor V \"Multi-faceted technical preparedness 8.9\": factor IV is smaller than factor V, against the ordering of factors by share. 12.7 would restore the order and bring the column to 97.6%; the printed value may be a misprint, but the source does not say. The entry's \"the skaters' jump in \"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\" (2.7% to 36.5%)\" relies on that value. Rest checked: all nine values and labels against image; sums 87.6 and 89.1; \"sharp rise\", \"combined as one factor (i.e. aerobic efficiency)\", \"anaerobic power increases slightly (factor III)\" across PDF 263-264; conclusion quote; glycolysis conflict cross-referenced correctly; baseline line 172. | (1) In When NOT add: \"Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against the order of factors by share (verified against the page image); 2.7 may be a misprint (12.7 would restore the order and bring the column to about 98%), but the source gives no correction, so treat the 2.7 value as unclear.\" (2) In When to apply replace the Inference with: \"Inference: the rise of \"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\" to the first factor at Master of Sport level (36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point; the Class 1 value for the same factor (2.7) is unclear, so the size of the change should not be quoted.\" |\n\n## `_book.md` (4.3 and 4.4 rows, newest batch note)","numLines":5,"startLine":45,"totalLines":81}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5cf477a7-55ed-4a4c-85c3-84886c9fc2e7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"