{"parentUuid":"1833110b-5a55-4263-bc45-087e3395e560","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVE2FYYUGAXRbFD2rs8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VopEcT7TAtMNLZEABhk2FM","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-2b_applied.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9330,"cache_read_input_tokens":59218,"output_tokens":216,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9330},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":216,"cache_read_input_tokens":59218,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9330,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9330,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VopEcT7TAtMNLZEABhk2FM":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-2b_applied.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffVE1zRBpTbUgjx5KpGu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"df6bae23-d47f-48ef-a8af-7622b2d5d24e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:09.524Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"df6bae23-d47f-48ef-a8af-7622b2d5d24e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VopEcT7TAtMNLZEABhk2FM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.2.4-4.2.9 (Ch4-2b), 20 entries\n2\t\n3\tFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-09-27. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-2b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES).\n4\tSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, text of PDF idx 32-35 and 233-253 extracted with PyMuPDF; page images rendered and opened for PDF 236 (Figs 4.11, 4.12), PDF 238 (Figs 4.13, 4.14), PDF 244 (the 4.2.6.2 heading position) and PDF 245 (Fig 4.16). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\n5\tScope: the 20 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-2b.txt`. `_book.md` not touched (the review asks for no change there). No new entries written.\n6\t\n7\t| file | what changed |\n8\t|---|---|\n9\t| claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate.md | Locator to PDF pp. 234-235 / printed 223-224. When NOT: \"corroborated only by a 1970 review\" replaced by the book's \"rather contradictory data\" (Verkhoshansky 1970 review, p.224). Added the duration-vs-intensity tension with both sides: H&M one-third threshold and the authors' 80-95% (PDF 33 / p.22), Korobkov et al \"approximately equivalent increases\" at 20-80% 1RM early in training (PDF 34 / p.23, partly agreeing with p.224), the book's 70-80% explosive protocol (PDF 237 / p.226), and Oranchuk 2019 as relayed by Natera in the Moyer entry. Added the 6 / 10 / 8 s hold-length cross-reference (PDF 237 / p.226). \"Why it works\" reworded so the no-mechanism statement is the authors' own, not an unlabelled writer claim. Quoted the \"fundamental conditioning factor\" sentence exactly. Linked [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]] and [[framework--isometric-training-variables-and-adaptations]] in body and Related. |\n10\t| claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases.md | Locator to PDF pp. 238-239 / printed 227-228. Added Clarke et al (1958) as the source of the temperature optimum, with Petrofsky & Phillips (1986) placing it near 27-28 C. Fig 4.13 description tightened after re-opening the image (about 0 kPa at 0% to about 20 kPa at 100%). \"Why it works\" now uses the book's wording (\"unable to meet the physiological demands\") instead of a paraphrased pressure-exceeds-perfusion mechanism. When to apply: recovery timeline scoped, as Inference, to recovery of endurance after a fatiguing sustained contraction. Example: plank, wall-sit and mid-thigh pull removed; kept as Inference with \"whether a given hold is under 10-15% of maximum is not shown in the source\". Added a note that the text's \"(Fig 4.14)\" pointer for temperature is a source oddity (Fig 4.14 plots endurance vs %MVC). |\n11\t| claim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular.md | No change needed. Review verdict GO; I re-checked the 15-degree band, Thepault-Mathieu numbers (54/25, 30/25, 25/7) and the \"should not be interpreted\" caution on PDF 237-238 / p.226-227 and found no error. Reciprocal-link suggestion below. |\n12\t| framework--isometric-contraction-categorization-voluntary-reflexive.md | Fig 4.12 sentence rewritten from the image: curves are labelled \"Slow Isometrics\" and \"Explosive Isometrics\"; the caption says reflexive explosive action is steeper still, with the caption quoted. When NOT: \"detailed experimental corroboration\" rescoped to the absolute-strength vs explosive-strength split (p.225). Added oscillatory isometrics with Soechting & Roberts 1975, 5 Hz, and Basmajian 1978 (PDF 235 / p.224), plus \"negative evaluations of this method are premature\" (PDF 236 / p.225). Recorded a source mismatch the review did not raise: the only evidence is for voluntarily produced 5 Hz jerks, while Fig 4.11 files oscillatory under reflexive (Inference line in When NOT). When to apply: \"reflexive (explosive or oscillatory)\" changed to reflexive explosive, per the text; \"clean-and-jerk catch\" changed to the book's \"clean-and-jerk\". Locators now carry PDF and printed pages. |\n13\t| heuristic--isometric-priming-and-external-internal-classification.md | \"Why it works\": reaction-time mechanism replaced with \"The authors give no mechanism for the 7% reaction-time figure\", plus a labelled Inference linking to the p.224 inhibition argument and noting it concerns force. When to apply: reframed as a progressive-overload step (p.229); \"before attempting a true max attempt\" removed. Example: invented 110-120% replaced with the book's own 120-150% shoulder support and quasi-isometric first degrees of a squat. Locator to PDF p. 240 / printed p. 229. Quoted the priming and \"advocated by their scientists\" wording. Rack/spotter safety sentence labelled Inference. |\n14\t| heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks.md | Recorded the unreconciled 6 s / 10 s / 8 s hold limits on PDF 237 / p.226 with the exact wording. Methods reworded to \"methods of execution ... recommended for developing absolute strength\". Cardiovascular sentence split: the source's \"may have a harmful effect ... in some individuals\", then \"Inference:\" for the risk-factor and pairing advice. |\n15\t| heuristic--loadless-training-broomstick-protocol.md | Kovalik attribution fixed: only the 5 sets are Kovalik's; the 3-10 s rest is \"research indicates\"; breathing, 10-15 s movement and 6-8 s hold are given without a source. When to apply points to \"the protocol given on PDF p. 241, printed p. 230\". Extensor-flexor reasoning labelled Inference. Added the book's stated corroboration (dynamic stretching against resistance, no citation) and the pushups/free-standing squats travel note. \"early-stage\" changed to the book's \"recuperating from musculoskeletal injuries\". Added one sentence on active visualisation (review Missing item 5, PDF 241 / p.230) with its proviso. |\n16\t| claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff.md | Title changed to \"... Adaptation Removes Both Within 2-3 Weeks While Eccentric Work Capacity Rises\". Hortobagyi sentence quoted and cross-referenced to the p.230 \"often contradictory\" statement and the contested sibling. Downhill-running application labelled Inference and flagged as rat data. Locator to PDF p. 242 / printed p. 231. \"over time\" deleted. Also removed the unsourced label \"(the repeated-bout effect)\" and replaced the unsourced \"microdamage that manifests as DOMS\" causal claim with the book's \"accompanied by\" wording. |\n17\t| claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md | Added the p.231 Hortobagyi side (quoted, PDF 242). When NOT now opens with the contested reason (p.230 vs p.231) and Handford et al 2024 (vault note, p.9 non-superiority statement, checked). Deleted \"consistent with modern eccentric tendon-loading protocols\"; replaced with the book's own \"training and rehabilitation implication\" and a note that tendinopathy use is an extension. \"small, heterogeneous samples\" changed to \"sample sizes not given in the text\"; \"populations\" changed to the book's training velocity, measurement velocity, muscle groups and specificity principle. |\n18\t| framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism.md | Locator to PDF pp. 242-243 / printed 231-232. \"most effective maintenance\" changed to the quoted \"highly effective maintenance of muscular strength\". Added the p.232 quadriceps-to-squat non-transfer and the untested-qualities / elite-competitor sentences. When NOT: recorded Thistle et al 1967 as \"established\" on p.232 vs \"neither proved nor ... universally accepted\" on p.235 (PDF 246). Also repointed the swimmer quote to the physics-limitation entry, where it actually sits (it was attributed to the testing-limitations entry). |\n19\t| heuristic--isokinetic-strength-ratios-vary-by-angle-and-velocity.md | Fig 4.16 convention added (90 degrees = full extension), with values re-read from the image (about 3.0 and 2.1 at 80 degrees; 1.5 near 50 degrees; y-axis 0.5-3.5) and the caption's angle sentence quoted. Figure attribution fixed: uncited Cybex data; Vorobyev supplies the 80:20 / 50:50 ratios, Stephens & Reid the gravity-correction critique. Recorded the same-page tension (Vorobyev targets \"should be\" vs \"meaningless\"). Eric's baseline split into its own paragraph quoting method-baseline line 111, with the RTP judgment on a separate Inference line. Locator to PDF pp. 244-245 / printed 233-234. \"Why it works\" now says the authors give no mechanism, with the averaging point labelled Inference. Added Inference notes on the reversed angle convention and on Vorobyev's named measurement point. Confidence left `supported` (see Disagreements). Added [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]] to Related. Frontmatter `chapter: \"4.2.6.1\"` changed to `\"4.2.6.2\"`, and \"4.2.6.2 Limitations of the Isokinetic Method\" added to the locator string: the rendered PDF 244 image shows the 4.2.6.2 heading at the top of the page, above \"Fundamental Biomechanics\" and \"Recommended Strength Ratios\" (the text extraction puts it at the foot of the page). |\n20\t| claim--isokinetic-devices-cannot-be-truly-isokinetic-physics-limitation.md | Title changed to \"... Isokinetic Tests Miss the Acceleration Phases Where the Authors Say Most Injuries Occur, and the Elastic-Energy Contribution of Natural Movement\". Injury-transition claim (a) and elastic-energy claim (b) separated in What it is, Why it works and When NOT, both quoted. Injury claim marked as the authors' assertion with no citation. When NOT: added the book's own use of isokinetic data (Fig 4.16, PDF 245 / p.234; Komi & Buskirk, PDF 241 / p.230) and a pointer to the contested sibling. Locator to PDF p. 244 / printed p. 233. Example: the unsourced sprinting-hamstring injury-concentration clause removed. Added [[framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism]] to Related. |\n21\t| heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md | Title changed to \"Three Grounds (Anatomy, Physiology, Specificity) on Which ...\"; \"six points\" changed to \"the itemised points\". Added the p.235 phase-restriction sentence (PDF 246) and the p.236 \"relatively useless\" sentence (PDF 247), both quoted; When to apply now says, per the authors, to confine isokinetic work to early general conditioning or rehabilitation. Contested reason now has pages: Fig 4.16 (PDF 245 / p.234), Komi & Buskirk (PDF 241 / p.230), Figs 2.8-2.9 (cited PDF 243 / p.232 and PDF 246 / p.235). \"Ostering\" changed to \"Osternig\". \"(see 4.3, not yet ingested in this pass)\" changed to \"(see 4.3)\". Locator changed from PDF pp. 245-247 / printed 234-236 to PDF pp. 244-247 / printed 233-236, because the Cybex/Orthotron introduction the entry summarises sits under the 4.2.6.2 heading on PDF 244. |\n22\t| claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method.md | Variant 2 sentence quoted verbatim (\"an equivalent affect on the improvement of speed-strength and absolute strength\") with the note that the comparator is unclear. When to apply: \"useful mainly for variety\" now an Inference conditional on the second reading. |\n23\t| heuristic--choice-of-muscle-training-regime-favor-combination.md | `confidence: supported` changed to `contested`. Added the p.231 \"premature ... any regime or combination of regimes\" statement with its three stated deficiencies (PDF 242), set against the p.237 \"reasonable certainty\" (PDF 248), in What it is and as the contested reason in When NOT. Noted that only Pletnev 1975 is a reported direct comparison. Title changed to \"... The Authors Judge Combining Regimes the Most Rational Approach\". \"(see 4.3, not yet ingested in this pass)\" changed to \"(see 4.3)\". \"nine corroborating citations\" changed to \"The corroborating citations\" (the list is nine author groups, ten works). \"Why it works\" reworded so it no longer asserts that combined regimes outperform. |\n24\t| framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines.md | Reversed sentence fixed: the book says the NFR machine-only facility gives the inferior multi-dimensional conditioning (quoted, PDF 249 / p.238). Ratov wording fixed to the book's \"supposed to imitate or train ... specific loads in the different regimes of muscular work (Ratov, 1976)\". \"to a single plane and pattern\" changed to the book's \"fixed bars and pivots\". Added the p.237 \"profoundly counterproductive for sport specific trainees\" sentence (PDF 248). |\n25\t| heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use.md | False Eric's baseline replaced. It now quotes method-baseline lines 59-60 (\"the standard CMJ family\" on ForceDecks; \"the isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks 'Mid-Thigh Pull' setting: 90-degree ankle/knee position\") and notes they are tagged Inferred (protocol). A separate Inference line says the seated calf raise is a fixed-angle isometric test, not NFR machine training. Injury-equivalence comparison marked as uncited in What it is and When NOT (p.239). Locator printed pp. 238-240 changed to 238-239. Wilke 1999 not inserted (see Disagreements); instead When NOT notes the disc figures rest on one cited source (Chaffin & Andersson 1984) and that the library holds no modern source on the point. |\n26\t| claim--nfr-machine-marketing-claims-examined.md | Title changed to \"The Authors Argue Variable-Resistance Machines' Core Marketing Premise Is a Physics Error, and That NFR Machines Need Many More Units to Match Free Weights\". Machine-count mechanism now reads \"The authors give no mechanism for the counts. Inference: ...\". Added an Inference caveat to When NOT: the Newtonian point does not rebut the torque-angle argument. When to apply points to that limit. The \"useful features\" sentence is now labelled Inference. |\n27\t| claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive.md | \"Usefully\" dropped from the title. When NOT now opens with the Class-2 peak (asymmetry declined with higher mastery, PDF 251 / p.240). Mechanism replaced with the book's \"could exacerbate the existing problem or cause new injuries\" plus a labelled Inference. The 10% finding is marked as research the authors do not cite. Eric's baseline now quotes method-baseline (\"optional symmetry metrics (L/R differences)\", tagged \"Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own\"), with the monitoring-not-correction judgment on its own Inference line. \"Soviet sprint-training research programme\" (not in the source) replaced by the classification levels the text gives. Linked Bishop 2016 and Callaway 2026 in Related (both resolve in the vault). |\n28\t| claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md | Title changed to \"'Muscle Isolation' Is Possible Only for Small Muscle Groups at Low Intensity; Heavy or Prolonged Resistance ...\". The EMG evidence is now quoted as \"minute muscle groups\". When to apply: rehab value separated from the EMG evidence, and limb-muscle isolation labelled Inference. Added \"or the greater the duration\" (quoted, with the restrained-joints clause), \"equal or exceed\", and the PNF overflow point. \"nearly as strongly\" changed to the quoted \"may be as strongly contracted as the triceps\". The irradiation linkage is now labelled Inference. |\n29\t\n30\t## Disagreements with the review\n31\t\n32\t1. **Ratio entry not raised to `contested`.** The review says \"consider\". I read the page: \"they have determined that this ratio ... should be nearer 80:20\" is Vorobyev's finding in reported speech (PDF 244 / p.233). The authors' own conclusion runs one way (\"meaningless to prescribe an optimal ratio for any joint\"). The brief's rule is X and not-X in the source's own voice, or another library source or modern evidence that disagrees. Neither applies: a grep of the vault for hamstring:quadriceps / H:Q ratio sources found none. I recorded the same-page tension in the body and left `supported`.\n33\t2. **Regime-choice entry set to `contested`**, as the review recommends. Both statements are the authors' own voice on the same question: \"to speak of anything definitive ... any regime or combination of regimes is premature\" (PDF 242 / p.231) and \"Today it can be ascertained with reasonable certainty ...\" (PDF 248 / p.237). This agrees with the review.\n34\t3. **Wilke et al 1999 not inserted** (NFR limitations). The reviewer marked it as to be verified. It is not in the vault (grep for \"Wilke\" found nothing), and a Zotero search (\"Wilke intradiscal pressure in vivo sitting standing\") returned no items. I left it out and added a neutral single-source caveat instead.\n35\t4. **Alfredson et al 1998 not inserted** (eccentric vs concentric). It is not in the vault. Zotero holds it only as a reference inside other papers (Malliaras 2013, Kjaer 2004, Douglas 2017), not as a held paper, so I could not confirm the \"slow\" characterisation. I deleted the unsupported clause, as the review requires, and added no citation.\n36\t5. **Oscillatory isometrics: an extra source mismatch recorded.** The review asks only for the Soechting & Roberts sentence. The page says \"the maximum tension that can be produced voluntarily\" during the 5 Hz jerks (PDF 235 / p.224), while Fig 4.11 (PDF 236 / p.225) files oscillatory under reflexive. I recorded both.\n37\t6. **Hettinger duration-vs-intensity: attribution refined.** The review attributes \"about one-third max rising to 80-95%\" to Hettinger & Muller. On PDF 33 / p.22 only the one-third threshold is cited to H&M (1953); the 80-95% rise is the authors' continuation (Verkhoshansky 1977 follows). PDF 34 / p.23 (Korobkov et al) also partly agrees with the p.224 inference for beginners. Both sides are written that way.\n38\t7. **Endurance entry: Fig 4.14 not cited for temperature.** The book's own \"(Fig 4.14)\" pointer for the temperature optimum (PDF 238 / p.227) points to a figure of endurance vs %MVC with no temperature data. I recorded this as a source oddity.\n39\t8. **Regional-specificity entry (GO): I added no link.** The review's suggested Moyer link is a reciprocal link for the vault note; it is listed below, not added here.\n40\t\n41\tMissing ideas (review \"Missing\" list): no new entries were written, per the task. Items 1 (oscillatory isometrics), 3 (research caveats), 4 (quadriceps-to-squat non-transfer) and 5 (active visualisation) now appear as sentences in existing entries. Item 2 (static vs dynamic contraction differ in the nervous system, PDF 239 / p.228) is not captured anywhere and is still open for a future entry.\n42\t\n43\t## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n44\t\n45\tFor the vault or other-book entries. Not edited here.\n46\t\n47\t- Vault Ch3 `claim--natural-asymmetry-caution-against-isokinetic-symmetry-correction.md`: replace \"Ch4.2.8 \\\"The Concept of Symmetric Training\\\" (explicitly deferred by the text itself, not yet ingested, future chapter).\" with: `Ch4.2.8 \"The Concept of Symmetric Training\": [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]].`\n48\t- Vault Ch2 `heuristic--isometric-dynamometry-misrepresents-dynamic-functional-strength.md`, Related: add `[[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]] (Ch4.2.6.2, the itemised anatomy, physiology and specificity case against isokinetic testing).`\n49\t- Vault Moyer `framework--isometric-training-variables-and-adaptations.md`, Related: add `[[claim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular]] (Supertraining, Thepault-Mathieu 1988: longer-length training spreads gains across the range, agreeing with Oranchuk 2019 as relayed here).` and `[[claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate]] (Supertraining, the original H&M protocol and the book's \"duration not magnitude\" inference, which this entry's intensity findings sit against).`\n50\t- Vault Ch1 `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus.md`, Related: add `[[claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate]] (Ch4.2.4, where the book says duration, not magnitude, is the fundamental isometric conditioning factor, in tension with this threshold rule).`\n51\t- Staging `claim--muscle-recruitment-consistency-and-proximal-distal-shift-with-load.md` already links the isolation entry; no change needed.\n52\t\n53\t## Checks\n54\t\n55\tLint, run from WT: `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 python scripts/lint_library.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`\n56\t\n57\t```\n58\tsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\n59\tTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\tThe one remaining issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside my edits. The first lint run also flagged \"round 2\" in the endurance entry: this was the substring in \"lies around 27-28\" inside a book quote. I paraphrased it (\"an optimum later placed near 27-28 Celsius\") and the flag cleared.\n63\t\n64\tOwn greps over the 20 scoped files, run from the book folder with `F=$(cat ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch4-2b.txt | tr -d '\\r')`:\n65\t\n66\t```\n67\t== em dashes not in section headers:        grep -nP '(?<!\\*\\* )—' $F\n68\t(end)\n69\t== em dashes in titles:                      grep -n '^# .*—' $F\n70\t(end)\n71\t== review-history words:                     grep -niE 'prior version|previous version|earlier draft|corrected|round[ -]?[12]\\b|reviewer|not yet ingested|NO-GO|fix pass|fixer|this revision' $F\n72\t(end)\n73\t== wrapped wikilinks:                        grep -n '\\[\\[[^]]*$' $F\n74\t(end)\n75\t== header count per file:\n76\tclaim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate.md 6\n77\tclaim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases.md 6\n78\tclaim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular.md 6\n79\tframework--isometric-contraction-categorization-voluntary-reflexive.md 6\n80\theuristic--isometric-priming-and-external-internal-classification.md 6\n81\theuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks.md 6\n82\theuristic--loadless-training-broomstick-protocol.md 6\n83\tclaim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff.md 6\n84\tclaim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md 6\n85\tframework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism.md 6\n86\theuristic--isokinetic-strength-ratios-vary-by-angle-and-velocity.md 6\n87\tclaim--isokinetic-devices-cannot-be-truly-isokinetic-physics-limitation.md 6\n88\theuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md 6\n89\tclaim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method.md 6\n90\theuristic--choice-of-muscle-training-regime-favor-combination.md 6\n91\tframework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines.md 6\n92\theuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use.md 6\n93\tclaim--nfr-machine-marketing-claims-examined.md 6\n94\tclaim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive.md 6\n95\tclaim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md 6\n96\t```\n97\t\n98\tLint was re-run after the last edit (the chapter-field and locator fix); output unchanged from the block above. Files use LF line endings (a grep for CR over the book folder found none).\n99\t\n100\tFor the promoter: this batch now carries 3 `contested` entries (eccentric-vs-concentric, isokinetic-testing-limitations, choice-of-regime), not 2. If `_book.md` keeps a confidence tally for this chapter, it needs updating; I did not touch `_book.md`.\n101\t\n102\tEvery wikilink target in the 20 files resolves in either the staging book folder or `SecondBrain/Knowledge` (a find over each basename printed no UNRESOLVED lines). \"Eric's baseline:\" lines appear in 3 files (ratios, NFR limitations, symmetry), and each quotes `docs/method-baseline.md` (lines 111, 59-60 and 61 respectively).\n103\t\n104\t## Round-2 corrections applied 2026-09-28 (from `review_supertraining_ch4-2b_opus_round2.md`, verbatim, by the orchestrator, not the reviewer or the round-1 fixer)\n105\t\n106\tAll 9 required corrections applied by exact string replacement; each Old string matched exactly once. Optional O1-O3 not applied.\n107\t\n108\t| File | What changed |\n109\t|---|---|\n110\t| claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases.md | C1: Fig 4.13 values re-read (about 15 kPa at 60%, 19 kPa at 80%, line ends near 100% at about 23 kPa). C7: Fig 4.15 named as the temperature figure (Clarke et al 1958, water-bath optimum about 18 C) and kept apart from the 27-28 C muscle optimum. C8: locator now \"Figs 4.13-4.15\". |\n111\t| heuristic--isokinetic-strength-ratios-vary-by-angle-and-velocity.md | C2: the text's 75:25 (3.0) and 68:32 (2.1) kept, and the figure's own reading at 80 degrees (about 2.7 and 2.2, PDF 245) recorded as not matching. |\n112\t| claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate.md | C3: \"partly agrees with the p.224 inference\" labelled Inference, with the %1RM caveat. |\n113\t| heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md | C4: \"most sporting actions\" -> \"many\". C5: isokinetic conditions exclude \"ballistic or discontinuous cocontractive actions\" (PDF 246, printed p. 235). |\n114\t| claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md | C6: no longer \"the same\" shift; the book's own link stated (PDF 229, printed p. 218). |\n115\t| _book.md | C9: Fig 4.15 added to the batch figure list. |\n116\t\n117\tChecks: `lint_library.py` on the Supertraining folder after applying: `_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]`, `TOTAL ISSUES: 1` (known false positive).\n118\t"}]},"uuid":"105099ab-f9da-4145-9117-b2afcb4be3cc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:09.674Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-2b_applied.md","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.2.4-4.2.9 (Ch4-2b), 20 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-09-27. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-2b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, text of PDF idx 32-35 and 233-253 extracted with PyMuPDF; page images rendered and opened for PDF 236 (Figs 4.11, 4.12), PDF 238 (Figs 4.13, 4.14), PDF 244 (the 4.2.6.2 heading position) and PDF 245 (Fig 4.16). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 20 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-2b.txt`. `_book.md` not touched (the review asks for no change there). No new entries written.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate.md | Locator to PDF pp. 234-235 / printed 223-224. When NOT: \"corroborated only by a 1970 review\" replaced by the book's \"rather contradictory data\" (Verkhoshansky 1970 review, p.224). Added the duration-vs-intensity tension with both sides: H&M one-third threshold and the authors' 80-95% (PDF 33 / p.22), Korobkov et al \"approximately equivalent increases\" at 20-80% 1RM early in training (PDF 34 / p.23, partly agreeing with p.224), the book's 70-80% explosive protocol (PDF 237 / p.226), and Oranchuk 2019 as relayed by Natera in the Moyer entry. Added the 6 / 10 / 8 s hold-length cross-reference (PDF 237 / p.226). \"Why it works\" reworded so the no-mechanism statement is the authors' own, not an unlabelled writer claim. Quoted the \"fundamental conditioning factor\" sentence exactly. Linked [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]] and [[framework--isometric-training-variables-and-adaptations]] in body and Related. |\n| claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases.md | Locator to PDF pp. 238-239 / printed 227-228. Added Clarke et al (1958) as the source of the temperature optimum, with Petrofsky & Phillips (1986) placing it near 27-28 C. Fig 4.13 description tightened after re-opening the image (about 0 kPa at 0% to about 20 kPa at 100%). \"Why it works\" now uses the book's wording (\"unable to meet the physiological demands\") instead of a paraphrased pressure-exceeds-perfusion mechanism. When to apply: recovery timeline scoped, as Inference, to recovery of endurance after a fatiguing sustained contraction. Example: plank, wall-sit and mid-thigh pull removed; kept as Inference with \"whether a given hold is under 10-15% of maximum is not shown in the source\". Added a note that the text's \"(Fig 4.14)\" pointer for temperature is a source oddity (Fig 4.14 plots endurance vs %MVC). |\n| claim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular.md | No change needed. Review verdict GO; I re-checked the 15-degree band, Thepault-Mathieu numbers (54/25, 30/25, 25/7) and the \"should not be interpreted\" caution on PDF 237-238 / p.226-227 and found no error. Reciprocal-link suggestion below. |\n| framework--isometric-contraction-categorization-voluntary-reflexive.md | Fig 4.12 sentence rewritten from the image: curves are labelled \"Slow Isometrics\" and \"Explosive Isometrics\"; the caption says reflexive explosive action is steeper still, with the caption quoted. When NOT: \"detailed experimental corroboration\" rescoped to the absolute-strength vs explosive-strength split (p.225). Added oscillatory isometrics with Soechting & Roberts 1975, 5 Hz, and Basmajian 1978 (PDF 235 / p.224), plus \"negative evaluations of this method are premature\" (PDF 236 / p.225). Recorded a source mismatch the review did not raise: the only evidence is for voluntarily produced 5 Hz jerks, while Fig 4.11 files oscillatory under reflexive (Inference line in When NOT). When to apply: \"reflexive (explosive or oscillatory)\" changed to reflexive explosive, per the text; \"clean-and-jerk catch\" changed to the book's \"clean-and-jerk\". Locators now carry PDF and printed pages. |\n| heuristic--isometric-priming-and-external-internal-classification.md | \"Why it works\": reaction-time mechanism replaced with \"The authors give no mechanism for the 7% reaction-time figure\", plus a labelled Inference linking to the p.224 inhibition argument and noting it concerns force. When to apply: reframed as a progressive-overload step (p.229); \"before attempting a true max attempt\" removed. Example: invented 110-120% replaced with the book's own 120-150% shoulder support and quasi-isometric first degrees of a squat. Locator to PDF p. 240 / printed p. 229. Quoted the priming and \"advocated by their scientists\" wording. Rack/spotter safety sentence labelled Inference. |\n| heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks.md | Recorded the unreconciled 6 s / 10 s / 8 s hold limits on PDF 237 / p.226 with the exact wording. Methods reworded to \"methods of execution ... recommended for developing absolute strength\". Cardiovascular sentence split: the source's \"may have a harmful effect ... in some individuals\", then \"Inference:\" for the risk-factor and pairing advice. |\n| heuristic--loadless-training-broomstick-protocol.md | Kovalik attribution fixed: only the 5 sets are Kovalik's; the 3-10 s rest is \"research indicates\"; breathing, 10-15 s movement and 6-8 s hold are given without a source. When to apply points to \"the protocol given on PDF p. 241, printed p. 230\". Extensor-flexor reasoning labelled Inference. Added the book's stated corroboration (dynamic stretching against resistance, no citation) and the pushups/free-standing squats travel note. \"early-stage\" changed to the book's \"recuperating from musculoskeletal injuries\". Added one sentence on active visualisation (review Missing item 5, PDF 241 / p.230) with its proviso. |\n| claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff.md | Title changed to \"... Adaptation Removes Both Within 2-3 Weeks While Eccentric Work Capacity Rises\". Hortobagyi sentence quoted and cross-referenced to the p.230 \"often contradictory\" statement and the contested sibling. Downhill-running application labelled Inference and flagged as rat data. Locator to PDF p. 242 / printed p. 231. \"over time\" deleted. Also removed the unsourced label \"(the repeated-bout effect)\" and replaced the unsourced \"microdamage that manifests as DOMS\" causal claim with the book's \"accompanied by\" wording. |\n| claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md | Added the p.231 Hortobagyi side (quoted, PDF 242). When NOT now opens with the contested reason (p.230 vs p.231) and Handford et al 2024 (vault note, p.9 non-superiority statement, checked). Deleted \"consistent with modern eccentric tendon-loading protocols\"; replaced with the book's own \"training and rehabilitation implication\" and a note that tendinopathy use is an extension. \"small, heterogeneous samples\" changed to \"sample sizes not given in the text\"; \"populations\" changed to the book's training velocity, measurement velocity, muscle groups and specificity principle. |\n| framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism.md | Locator to PDF pp. 242-243 / printed 231-232. \"most effective maintenance\" changed to the quoted \"highly effective maintenance of muscular strength\". Added the p.232 quadriceps-to-squat non-transfer and the untested-qualities / elite-competitor sentences. When NOT: recorded Thistle et al 1967 as \"established\" on p.232 vs \"neither proved nor ... universally accepted\" on p.235 (PDF 246). Also repointed the swimmer quote to the physics-limitation entry, where it actually sits (it was attributed to the testing-limitations entry). |\n| heuristic--isokinetic-strength-ratios-vary-by-angle-and-velocity.md | Fig 4.16 convention added (90 degrees = full extension), with values re-read from the image (about 3.0 and 2.1 at 80 degrees; 1.5 near 50 degrees; y-axis 0.5-3.5) and the caption's angle sentence quoted. Figure attribution fixed: uncited Cybex data; Vorobyev supplies the 80:20 / 50:50 ratios, Stephens & Reid the gravity-correction critique. Recorded the same-page tension (Vorobyev targets \"should be\" vs \"meaningless\"). Eric's baseline split into its own paragraph quoting method-baseline line 111, with the RTP judgment on a separate Inference line. Locator to PDF pp. 244-245 / printed 233-234. \"Why it works\" now says the authors give no mechanism, with the averaging point labelled Inference. Added Inference notes on the reversed angle convention and on Vorobyev's named measurement point. Confidence left `supported` (see Disagreements). Added [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]] to Related. Frontmatter `chapter: \"4.2.6.1\"` changed to `\"4.2.6.2\"`, and \"4.2.6.2 Limitations of the Isokinetic Method\" added to the locator string: the rendered PDF 244 image shows the 4.2.6.2 heading at the top of the page, above \"Fundamental Biomechanics\" and \"Recommended Strength Ratios\" (the text extraction puts it at the foot of the page). |\n| claim--isokinetic-devices-cannot-be-truly-isokinetic-physics-limitation.md | Title changed to \"... Isokinetic Tests Miss the Acceleration Phases Where the Authors Say Most Injuries Occur, and the Elastic-Energy Contribution of Natural Movement\". Injury-transition claim (a) and elastic-energy claim (b) separated in What it is, Why it works and When NOT, both quoted. Injury claim marked as the authors' assertion with no citation. When NOT: added the book's own use of isokinetic data (Fig 4.16, PDF 245 / p.234; Komi & Buskirk, PDF 241 / p.230) and a pointer to the contested sibling. Locator to PDF p. 244 / printed p. 233. Example: the unsourced sprinting-hamstring injury-concentration clause removed. Added [[framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism]] to Related. |\n| heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md | Title changed to \"Three Grounds (Anatomy, Physiology, Specificity) on Which ...\"; \"six points\" changed to \"the itemised points\". Added the p.235 phase-restriction sentence (PDF 246) and the p.236 \"relatively useless\" sentence (PDF 247), both quoted; When to apply now says, per the authors, to confine isokinetic work to early general conditioning or rehabilitation. Contested reason now has pages: Fig 4.16 (PDF 245 / p.234), Komi & Buskirk (PDF 241 / p.230), Figs 2.8-2.9 (cited PDF 243 / p.232 and PDF 246 / p.235). \"Ostering\" changed to \"Osternig\". \"(see 4.3, not yet ingested in this pass)\" changed to \"(see 4.3)\". Locator changed from PDF pp. 245-247 / printed 234-236 to PDF pp. 244-247 / printed 233-236, because the Cybex/Orthotron introduction the entry summarises sits under the 4.2.6.2 heading on PDF 244. |\n| claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method.md | Variant 2 sentence quoted verbatim (\"an equivalent affect on the improvement of speed-strength and absolute strength\") with the note that the comparator is unclear. When to apply: \"useful mainly for variety\" now an Inference conditional on the second reading. |\n| heuristic--choice-of-muscle-training-regime-favor-combination.md | `confidence: supported` changed to `contested`. Added the p.231 \"premature ... any regime or combination of regimes\" statement with its three stated deficiencies (PDF 242), set against the p.237 \"reasonable certainty\" (PDF 248), in What it is and as the contested reason in When NOT. Noted that only Pletnev 1975 is a reported direct comparison. Title changed to \"... The Authors Judge Combining Regimes the Most Rational Approach\". \"(see 4.3, not yet ingested in this pass)\" changed to \"(see 4.3)\". \"nine corroborating citations\" changed to \"The corroborating citations\" (the list is nine author groups, ten works). \"Why it works\" reworded so it no longer asserts that combined regimes outperform. |\n| framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines.md | Reversed sentence fixed: the book says the NFR machine-only facility gives the inferior multi-dimensional conditioning (quoted, PDF 249 / p.238). Ratov wording fixed to the book's \"supposed to imitate or train ... specific loads in the different regimes of muscular work (Ratov, 1976)\". \"to a single plane and pattern\" changed to the book's \"fixed bars and pivots\". Added the p.237 \"profoundly counterproductive for sport specific trainees\" sentence (PDF 248). |\n| heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use.md | False Eric's baseline replaced. It now quotes method-baseline lines 59-60 (\"the standard CMJ family\" on ForceDecks; \"the isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks 'Mid-Thigh Pull' setting: 90-degree ankle/knee position\") and notes they are tagged Inferred (protocol). A separate Inference line says the seated calf raise is a fixed-angle isometric test, not NFR machine training. Injury-equivalence comparison marked as uncited in What it is and When NOT (p.239). Locator printed pp. 238-240 changed to 238-239. Wilke 1999 not inserted (see Disagreements); instead When NOT notes the disc figures rest on one cited source (Chaffin & Andersson 1984) and that the library holds no modern source on the point. |\n| claim--nfr-machine-marketing-claims-examined.md | Title changed to \"The Authors Argue Variable-Resistance Machines' Core Marketing Premise Is a Physics Error, and That NFR Machines Need Many More Units to Match Free Weights\". Machine-count mechanism now reads \"The authors give no mechanism for the counts. Inference: ...\". Added an Inference caveat to When NOT: the Newtonian point does not rebut the torque-angle argument. When to apply points to that limit. The \"useful features\" sentence is now labelled Inference. |\n| claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive.md | \"Usefully\" dropped from the title. When NOT now opens with the Class-2 peak (asymmetry declined with higher mastery, PDF 251 / p.240). Mechanism replaced with the book's \"could exacerbate the existing problem or cause new injuries\" plus a labelled Inference. The 10% finding is marked as research the authors do not cite. Eric's baseline now quotes method-baseline (\"optional symmetry metrics (L/R differences)\", tagged \"Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own\"), with the monitoring-not-correction judgment on its own Inference line. \"Soviet sprint-training research programme\" (not in the source) replaced by the classification levels the text gives. Linked Bishop 2016 and Callaway 2026 in Related (both resolve in the vault). |\n| claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md | Title changed to \"'Muscle Isolation' Is Possible Only for Small Muscle Groups at Low Intensity; Heavy or Prolonged Resistance ...\". The EMG evidence is now quoted as \"minute muscle groups\". When to apply: rehab value separated from the EMG evidence, and limb-muscle isolation labelled Inference. Added \"or the greater the duration\" (quoted, with the restrained-joints clause), \"equal or exceed\", and the PNF overflow point. \"nearly as strongly\" changed to the quoted \"may be as strongly contracted as the triceps\". The irradiation linkage is now labelled Inference. |\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Ratio entry not raised to `contested`.** The review says \"consider\". I read the page: \"they have determined that this ratio ... should be nearer 80:20\" is Vorobyev's finding in reported speech (PDF 244 / p.233). The authors' own conclusion runs one way (\"meaningless to prescribe an optimal ratio for any joint\"). The brief's rule is X and not-X in the source's own voice, or another library source or modern evidence that disagrees. Neither applies: a grep of the vault for hamstring:quadriceps / H:Q ratio sources found none. I recorded the same-page tension in the body and left `supported`.\n2. **Regime-choice entry set to `contested`**, as the review recommends. Both statements are the authors' own voice on the same question: \"to speak of anything definitive ... any regime or combination of regimes is premature\" (PDF 242 / p.231) and \"Today it can be ascertained with reasonable certainty ...\" (PDF 248 / p.237). This agrees with the review.\n3. **Wilke et al 1999 not inserted** (NFR limitations). The reviewer marked it as to be verified. It is not in the vault (grep for \"Wilke\" found nothing), and a Zotero search (\"Wilke intradiscal pressure in vivo sitting standing\") returned no items. I left it out and added a neutral single-source caveat instead.\n4. **Alfredson et al 1998 not inserted** (eccentric vs concentric). It is not in the vault. Zotero holds it only as a reference inside other papers (Malliaras 2013, Kjaer 2004, Douglas 2017), not as a held paper, so I could not confirm the \"slow\" characterisation. I deleted the unsupported clause, as the review requires, and added no citation.\n5. **Oscillatory isometrics: an extra source mismatch recorded.** The review asks only for the Soechting & Roberts sentence. The page says \"the maximum tension that can be produced voluntarily\" during the 5 Hz jerks (PDF 235 / p.224), while Fig 4.11 (PDF 236 / p.225) files oscillatory under reflexive. I recorded both.\n6. **Hettinger duration-vs-intensity: attribution refined.** The review attributes \"about one-third max rising to 80-95%\" to Hettinger & Muller. On PDF 33 / p.22 only the one-third threshold is cited to H&M (1953); the 80-95% rise is the authors' continuation (Verkhoshansky 1977 follows). PDF 34 / p.23 (Korobkov et al) also partly agrees with the p.224 inference for beginners. Both sides are written that way.\n7. **Endurance entry: Fig 4.14 not cited for temperature.** The book's own \"(Fig 4.14)\" pointer for the temperature optimum (PDF 238 / p.227) points to a figure of endurance vs %MVC with no temperature data. I recorded this as a source oddity.\n8. **Regional-specificity entry (GO): I added no link.** The review's suggested Moyer link is a reciprocal link for the vault note; it is listed below, not added here.\n\nMissing ideas (review \"Missing\" list): no new entries were written, per the task. Items 1 (oscillatory isometrics), 3 (research caveats), 4 (quadriceps-to-squat non-transfer) and 5 (active visualisation) now appear as sentences in existing entries. Item 2 (static vs dynamic contraction differ in the nervous system, PDF 239 / p.228) is not captured anywhere and is still open for a future entry.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n\nFor the vault or other-book entries. Not edited here.\n\n- Vault Ch3 `claim--natural-asymmetry-caution-against-isokinetic-symmetry-correction.md`: replace \"Ch4.2.8 \\\"The Concept of Symmetric Training\\\" (explicitly deferred by the text itself, not yet ingested, future chapter).\" with: `Ch4.2.8 \"The Concept of Symmetric Training\": [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]].`\n- Vault Ch2 `heuristic--isometric-dynamometry-misrepresents-dynamic-functional-strength.md`, Related: add `[[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]] (Ch4.2.6.2, the itemised anatomy, physiology and specificity case against isokinetic testing).`\n- Vault Moyer `framework--isometric-training-variables-and-adaptations.md`, Related: add `[[claim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular]] (Supertraining, Thepault-Mathieu 1988: longer-length training spreads gains across the range, agreeing with Oranchuk 2019 as relayed here).` and `[[claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate]] (Supertraining, the original H&M protocol and the book's \"duration not magnitude\" inference, which this entry's intensity findings sit against).`\n- Vault Ch1 `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus.md`, Related: add `[[claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate]] (Ch4.2.4, where the book says duration, not magnitude, is the fundamental isometric conditioning factor, in tension with this threshold rule).`\n- Staging `claim--muscle-recruitment-consistency-and-proximal-distal-shift-with-load.md` already links the isolation entry; no change needed.\n\n## Checks\n\nLint, run from WT: `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 python scripts/lint_library.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`\n\n```\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nThe one remaining issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside my edits. The first lint run also flagged \"round 2\" in the endurance entry: this was the substring in \"lies around 27-28\" inside a book quote. I paraphrased it (\"an optimum later placed near 27-28 Celsius\") and the flag cleared.\n\nOwn greps over the 20 scoped files, run from the book folder with `F=$(cat ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch4-2b.txt | tr -d '\\r')`:\n\n```\n== em dashes not in section headers:        grep -nP '(?<!\\*\\* )—' $F\n(end)\n== em dashes in titles:                      grep -n '^# .*—' $F\n(end)\n== review-history words:                     grep -niE 'prior version|previous version|earlier draft|corrected|round[ -]?[12]\\b|reviewer|not yet ingested|NO-GO|fix pass|fixer|this revision' $F\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks:                        grep -n '\\[\\[[^]]*$' $F\n(end)\n== header count per file:\nclaim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate.md 6\nclaim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases.md 6\nclaim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular.md 6\nframework--isometric-contraction-categorization-voluntary-reflexive.md 6\nheuristic--isometric-priming-and-external-internal-classification.md 6\nheuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks.md 6\nheuristic--loadless-training-broomstick-protocol.md 6\nclaim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff.md 6\nclaim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md 6\nframework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism.md 6\nheuristic--isokinetic-strength-ratios-vary-by-angle-and-velocity.md 6\nclaim--isokinetic-devices-cannot-be-truly-isokinetic-physics-limitation.md 6\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md 6\nclaim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method.md 6\nheuristic--choice-of-muscle-training-regime-favor-combination.md 6\nframework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines.md 6\nheuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use.md 6\nclaim--nfr-machine-marketing-claims-examined.md 6\nclaim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive.md 6\nclaim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md 6\n```\n\nLint was re-run after the last edit (the chapter-field and locator fix); output unchanged from the block above. Files use LF line endings (a grep for CR over the book folder found none).\n\nFor the promoter: this batch now carries 3 `contested` entries (eccentric-vs-concentric, isokinetic-testing-limitations, choice-of-regime), not 2. If `_book.md` keeps a confidence tally for this chapter, it needs updating; I did not touch `_book.md`.\n\nEvery wikilink target in the 20 files resolves in either the staging book folder or `SecondBrain/Knowledge` (a find over each basename printed no UNRESOLVED lines). \"Eric's baseline:\" lines appear in 3 files (ratios, NFR limitations, symmetry), and each quotes `docs/method-baseline.md` (lines 111, 59-60 and 61 respectively).\n\n## Round-2 corrections applied 2026-09-28 (from `review_supertraining_ch4-2b_opus_round2.md`, verbatim, by the orchestrator, not the reviewer or the round-1 fixer)\n\nAll 9 required corrections applied by exact string replacement; each Old string matched exactly once. Optional O1-O3 not applied.\n\n| File | What changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases.md | C1: Fig 4.13 values re-read (about 15 kPa at 60%, 19 kPa at 80%, line ends near 100% at about 23 kPa). C7: Fig 4.15 named as the temperature figure (Clarke et al 1958, water-bath optimum about 18 C) and kept apart from the 27-28 C muscle optimum. C8: locator now \"Figs 4.13-4.15\". |\n| heuristic--isokinetic-strength-ratios-vary-by-angle-and-velocity.md | C2: the text's 75:25 (3.0) and 68:32 (2.1) kept, and the figure's own reading at 80 degrees (about 2.7 and 2.2, PDF 245) recorded as not matching. |\n| claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate.md | C3: \"partly agrees with the p.224 inference\" labelled Inference, with the %1RM caveat. |\n| heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md | C4: \"most sporting actions\" -> \"many\". C5: isokinetic conditions exclude \"ballistic or discontinuous cocontractive actions\" (PDF 246, printed p. 235). |\n| claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md | C6: no longer \"the same\" shift; the book's own link stated (PDF 229, printed p. 218). |\n| _book.md | C9: Fig 4.15 added to the batch figure list. |\n\nChecks: `lint_library.py` on the Supertraining folder after applying: `_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]`, `TOTAL ISSUES: 1` (known false positive).\n","numLines":118,"startLine":1,"totalLines":118}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"df6bae23-d47f-48ef-a8af-7622b2d5d24e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
