{"parentUuid":"f24eb1d9-6371-416c-9987-e58aac01c34b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVEPtJV3ZJKvaycR6RV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TANFBvKt8rGdvLZoGrP9cf","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; cat claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md; echo; echo \"#####\"; grep -n \"power-equals\" *.md","description":"Read the power entry and find links to it"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1434,"cache_read_input_tokens":80317,"output_tokens":179,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1434},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":179,"cache_read_input_tokens":80317,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1434,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1434,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TANFBvKt8rGdvLZoGrP9cf":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; cat claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md; echo; echo \"#####\"; grep -n \"power-equals\" *.md","description":"Read the power entry and find links to it"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffVEPcC4iUPwtZmuuraS","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c8c58b53-7396-4533-9c15-47542efb2635","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:14.565Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"c8c58b53-7396-4533-9c15-47542efb2635","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TANFBvKt8rGdvLZoGrP9cf","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.3.4\"\ntopic: power formula and force-time analysis\ncategory: [biomechanics, power, rate-of-force-development, testing]\nconfidence: proven\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# P = F x V Holds Only When Force Is Constant; Explosive Actions Need the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\n\n**What it is** — Scope of the `proven` rating: only the mathematical point that P = dW/dt reduces to P = F.V when force is constant. The claim that \"many strength training texts\" misuse it, and the advice on force plates and video, are the authors' judgment. The authors define power as the instantaneous rate of doing work, P = dW/dt, with work W = ∫F(t).ds, and note that \"if F is constant over the interval concerned\" this reduces to \"the familiar basic expression for power\": P = F.V, where V is the velocity of the object (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246; the equations are images, verified against the page image). In italics: \"this basic definition is applied without qualification in many strength training texts and can lead to serious misunderstanding of training theory and methods.\" When force is not constant, one must use the general form (equation 4.1) and look at how force and velocity vary over time, \"most conveniently\" from F-V, F-t and V-t graphs. \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone.\" In plyometric actions \"the force changes very rapidly as a complicated transient spike function and cannot be regarded as a constant throughout the rebound phase,\" so \"it is better to rely on a force plate or high speed video methods to analyse the movement than to attempt to apply elementary biomechanical calculations that will inevitably produce grave errors.\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246.)\n\n**Why it works** — This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case of P = dW/dt with constant force, so using it with a time-varying force gives a wrong instantaneous picture. Scope of `proven`: the mathematical relation only (P = dW/dt reduces to F.V under constant force). The practical judgment that \"many strength training texts\" misuse it is the authors' assessment, with no citations.\n\n**When to apply** — When reporting or comparing power or explosive strength: use instantaneous force-time and velocity-time data rather than one average force times one average velocity, and read the shape of the force-time curve, not just its peak or mean. For plyometric and rebound actions, measure on a force plate or with high-speed video. Eric's baseline: the force-plate reporting framework adds \"'fluffy' metrics (peak power, average power, RFD) only with a specific, defensible rationale.\" Tagged **Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 79). Inference: the authors' point gives one reason for that caution: single power numbers hide how force changed over the action.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Source oddities in the equations (verified against the page image, PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246): the text introduces equation (4.1), P = d/dt ∫F(t).ds, with \"if F is constant over the interval concerned, we obtain,\" although (4.1) is the general form and the constant-force form is the one beside it, P = F.d/dt ∫ds; and the impulse is written F.Δt \"where s is the displacement,\" though s does not appear in F.Δt. The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose. The instruction to use force plates is general and gives no sampling rate or analysis method.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: a plyometric rebound, where force is a transient spike, so elementary average-force calculations \"will inevitably produce grave errors\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]], [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]] (Ch1), [[framework--force-time-curve-adaptation-pattern-by-movement-type]], [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]], [[framework--four-component-model-of-explosive-force]].\n\n#####\nheuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md:26:**Related** — [[claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]], [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]], [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--explosive-strength-deficit-and-rate-of-force-development-indices]].","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"40de0763-aa71-44d5-bad6-85b24df80e2e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:14.889Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.3.4\"\ntopic: power formula and force-time analysis\ncategory: [biomechanics, power, rate-of-force-development, testing]\nconfidence: proven\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# P = F x V Holds Only When Force Is Constant; Explosive Actions Need the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\n\n**What it is** — Scope of the `proven` rating: only the mathematical point that P = dW/dt reduces to P = F.V when force is constant. The claim that \"many strength training texts\" misuse it, and the advice on force plates and video, are the authors' judgment. The authors define power as the instantaneous rate of doing work, P = dW/dt, with work W = ∫F(t).ds, and note that \"if F is constant over the interval concerned\" this reduces to \"the familiar basic expression for power\": P = F.V, where V is the velocity of the object (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246; the equations are images, verified against the page image). In italics: \"this basic definition is applied without qualification in many strength training texts and can lead to serious misunderstanding of training theory and methods.\" When force is not constant, one must use the general form (equation 4.1) and look at how force and velocity vary over time, \"most conveniently\" from F-V, F-t and V-t graphs. \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone.\" In plyometric actions \"the force changes very rapidly as a complicated transient spike function and cannot be regarded as a constant throughout the rebound phase,\" so \"it is better to rely on a force plate or high speed video methods to analyse the movement than to attempt to apply elementary biomechanical calculations that will inevitably produce grave errors.\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246.)\n\n**Why it works** — This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case of P = dW/dt with constant force, so using it with a time-varying force gives a wrong instantaneous picture. Scope of `proven`: the mathematical relation only (P = dW/dt reduces to F.V under constant force). The practical judgment that \"many strength training texts\" misuse it is the authors' assessment, with no citations.\n\n**When to apply** — When reporting or comparing power or explosive strength: use instantaneous force-time and velocity-time data rather than one average force times one average velocity, and read the shape of the force-time curve, not just its peak or mean. For plyometric and rebound actions, measure on a force plate or with high-speed video. Eric's baseline: the force-plate reporting framework adds \"'fluffy' metrics (peak power, average power, RFD) only with a specific, defensible rationale.\" Tagged **Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 79). Inference: the authors' point gives one reason for that caution: single power numbers hide how force changed over the action.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Source oddities in the equations (verified against the page image, PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246): the text introduces equation (4.1), P = d/dt ∫F(t).ds, with \"if F is constant over the interval concerned, we obtain,\" although (4.1) is the general form and the constant-force form is the one beside it, P = F.d/dt ∫ds; and the impulse is written F.Δt \"where s is the displacement,\" though s does not appear in F.Δt. The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose. The instruction to use force plates is general and gives no sampling rate or analysis method.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: a plyometric rebound, where force is a transient spike, so elementary average-force calculations \"will inevitably produce grave errors\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]], [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]] (Ch1), [[framework--force-time-curve-adaptation-pattern-by-movement-type]], [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]], [[framework--four-component-model-of-explosive-force]].\n\n#####\nheuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md:26:**Related** — [[claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]], [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]], [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--explosive-strength-deficit-and-rate-of-force-development-indices]].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c8c58b53-7396-4533-9c15-47542efb2635","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
