{"parentUuid":"29019b95-fd20-4e02-8632-7e538d0c497d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JBpfK6yuCCRKLcaMvFqU6J","type":"tool_result","content":"29:**When NOT to apply** — The criteria select special strength exercises; the authors say in the opening of the next chapter that this selection is \"only part of the process\" and that methods are a separate task (cross-reference only, PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They are not an argument against general preparation: the text gives no rule here for how much general work an athlete needs. The authors offer the criteria as reasoned principles with illustrative examples, not as the result of controlled transfer studies, so a high correspondence score does not by itself prove that an exercise will transfer. Inference: when the criteria conflict (for example, matching amplitude may require an awkward position that breaks the regime of work, as the authors' own Fig 4.22 discussion shows, PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245), the text gives no ranking among them.\n---\n28:**Why it works** — The physics the authors give (drag rising with the square of velocity) explains why parachute size and running speed together set the load: drag in fluid flow scaling with velocity squared is standard fluid mechanics. Inference: that physical relation is the `proven` core here; the size-to-quality mapping, distances, frequencies and progression are the authors' practice recommendations with no cited trial, so the entry as a whole is `supported`. The authors give no mechanism for why the quick-release contrast enhances stride frequency.\n---\n25:**When to apply** — When choosing between parachute, sled and worn-weight resistance: if vertical loading is the concern (heavy athletes, athletes returning from lower-limb injury), the authors' argument favours the parachute; if preserving flight-phase and landing mechanics is the concern (hurdlers, jumpers), it counts against it. Eric's baseline: Dynamic Correspondence governs \"whether an exercise actually transfers.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103). Inference: a coach weighing a parachute block can use these two limits as the checklist for what to watch on film.\n---\n18:The authors also show a partner exercise (Fig 4.22, verified against the page image: one partner on the athlete's shoulders, another pulling the athlete's back leg, the athlete against wall bars) where trying to meet the criteria gives \"incongruous and artificial\" positions. Its caption says the accentuated regions \"can be incorrectly resisted and posture can be inappropriately held\"; the text says it \"may be appropriate if applied carefully,\" but incorrect positioning \"can reduce its effectiveness markedly\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245).\n---\n20:**When to apply** — After an exercise passes the movement criteria (amplitude, direction, accentuated region), set its loading parameters from the sport's regime: repeated low-resistance fast actions call for light loads, fast tempo and many repetitions; single maximal actions against a fixed implement call for heavier loads, few repetitions and full recovery. Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk), replacing the loose and often-misapplied idea of 'sport specific.'\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript + slides)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102). Inference: this criterion adds that position on that spectrum depends on how an exercise is loaded, not only which exercise it is.\n---\n30:**When to apply** — For jumpers and athletes whose key action is a planted, high-load take-off: as qualification rises, give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position and less to plantarflexor power. More generally, the authors' rule is to plan from \"the general trends of change in the factor structure ... with increase in proficiency, as well as the individual peculiarities of a specific athlete\" (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253). Eric's baseline: isometric mid-thigh pull peak force enters the DSI used to choose between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 76). Inference: these data are a reason to keep an isometric knee-extension measure for jumpers, but they say nothing about the DSI ratio itself.\n---\n23:| IV | Energetic efficiency of executing specific work | 2.7 | Aerobic efficiency | 10.9 |\n30:**When to apply** — When an athlete's aerobic markers are already high but performance stalls: shift emphasis toward economy of the specific work, technical preparedness and anaerobic power, as the Master of Sport structure suggests. Eric's baseline: \"Movement efficiency (skill) is treated as directly reducing energy cost.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 172). Inference: the skaters' jump in \"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\" (2.7% to 36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point.\n\nclaim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max.md:23:- Raise VO2 max, or not: \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max, and in particular, to create some reserve of aerobic power by the end of the preparatory period\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249), against \"effectiveness of training does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\nclaim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains.md:16:**What it is** — \"Recently, haemodynamic factors have been shown to play an important role in the functional specialisation of the body during endurance work\" (nine citations). In italics: \"The redistribution of blood and an increase in circulation to the working muscles contributes to satisfying the oxygen requirements of the muscles and the removal of anaerobic metabolites.\" This \"Differentiation of the vesicular reaction providing effective redistribution of blood flow\" (Inference: \"vesicular\" is probably meant as \"vascular\") develops in the competition period \"as a result of the extensive aerobic loading executed during the preparatory period.\" In 25 km road cycling, improved performance came with a fall in VO2 max in the competition period while \"peak blood flow to the lower extremities increases,\" and cardiac output also fell (Stepochkin et al 1970). (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image.) \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\": while VO2 max falls somewhat in the competition period, \"OBLA can rise\" (Zatsiorsky et al 1974; Sirenko 1979; Nurmekivan 1974). In qualified skaters the mean change in VO2 max in the competition period is \"5-10%,\" while local blood flow rises \"much more significantly (50-250%)\" (Mellenberg 1981). The authors conclude that training effectiveness \"does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport,\" and that, with the muscles' strength and oxidative qualities, \"the redistribution of bloodflow and improved local vesicular reaction are important conditions for developing local muscle endurance.\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250, verified against the page image.) Because these conditions lessen dependence on VO2 max, the assumed \"antagonism in endurance sport between the development of the aerobic and anaerobic mechanisms must be questioned (Mellenberg & Khvan, 1982)\" (PDF pp. 260-261 / printed pp. 249-250).\nclaim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths.md:29:threshold (or turnpoint) or OBLA (Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation)\" (printed 78). (PDF\nclaim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths.md:44:irrelevant to training; the blood lactate threshold (OBLA) remains a legitimate, useful","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2074a730-7e71-4f40-86c6-68e7849040ce","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:38.866Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"29:**When NOT to apply** — The criteria select special strength exercises; the authors say in the opening of the next chapter that this selection is \"only part of the process\" and that methods are a separate task (cross-reference only, PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They are not an argument against general preparation: the text gives no rule here for how much general work an athlete needs. The authors offer the criteria as reasoned principles with illustrative examples, not as the result of controlled transfer studies, so a high correspondence score does not by itself prove that an exercise will transfer. Inference: when the criteria conflict (for example, matching amplitude may require an awkward position that breaks the regime of work, as the authors' own Fig 4.22 discussion shows, PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245), the text gives no ranking among them.\n---\n28:**Why it works** — The physics the authors give (drag rising with the square of velocity) explains why parachute size and running speed together set the load: drag in fluid flow scaling with velocity squared is standard fluid mechanics. Inference: that physical relation is the `proven` core here; the size-to-quality mapping, distances, frequencies and progression are the authors' practice recommendations with no cited trial, so the entry as a whole is `supported`. The authors give no mechanism for why the quick-release contrast enhances stride frequency.\n---\n25:**When to apply** — When choosing between parachute, sled and worn-weight resistance: if vertical loading is the concern (heavy athletes, athletes returning from lower-limb injury), the authors' argument favours the parachute; if preserving flight-phase and landing mechanics is the concern (hurdlers, jumpers), it counts against it. Eric's baseline: Dynamic Correspondence governs \"whether an exercise actually transfers.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103). Inference: a coach weighing a parachute block can use these two limits as the checklist for what to watch on film.\n---\n18:The authors also show a partner exercise (Fig 4.22, verified against the page image: one partner on the athlete's shoulders, another pulling the athlete's back leg, the athlete against wall bars) where trying to meet the criteria gives \"incongruous and artificial\" positions. Its caption says the accentuated regions \"can be incorrectly resisted and posture can be inappropriately held\"; the text says it \"may be appropriate if applied carefully,\" but incorrect positioning \"can reduce its effectiveness markedly\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245).\n---\n20:**When to apply** — After an exercise passes the movement criteria (amplitude, direction, accentuated region), set its loading parameters from the sport's regime: repeated low-resistance fast actions call for light loads, fast tempo and many repetitions; single maximal actions against a fixed implement call for heavier loads, few repetitions and full recovery. Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk), replacing the loose and often-misapplied idea of 'sport specific.'\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript + slides)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102). Inference: this criterion adds that position on that spectrum depends on how an exercise is loaded, not only which exercise it is.\n---\n30:**When to apply** — For jumpers and athletes whose key action is a planted, high-load take-off: as qualification rises, give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position and less to plantarflexor power. More generally, the authors' rule is to plan from \"the general trends of change in the factor structure ... with increase in proficiency, as well as the individual peculiarities of a specific athlete\" (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253). Eric's baseline: isometric mid-thigh pull peak force enters the DSI used to choose between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 76). Inference: these data are a reason to keep an isometric knee-extension measure for jumpers, but they say nothing about the DSI ratio itself.\n---\n23:| IV | Energetic efficiency of executing specific work | 2.7 | Aerobic efficiency | 10.9 |\n30:**When to apply** — When an athlete's aerobic markers are already high but performance stalls: shift emphasis toward economy of the specific work, technical preparedness and anaerobic power, as the Master of Sport structure suggests. Eric's baseline: \"Movement efficiency (skill) is treated as directly reducing energy cost.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 172). Inference: the skaters' jump in \"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\" (2.7% to 36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point.\n\nclaim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max.md:23:- Raise VO2 max, or not: \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max, and in particular, to create some reserve of aerobic power by the end of the preparatory period\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249), against \"effectiveness of training does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\nclaim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains.md:16:**What it is** — \"Recently, haemodynamic factors have been shown to play an important role in the functional specialisation of the body during endurance work\" (nine citations). In italics: \"The redistribution of blood and an increase in circulation to the working muscles contributes to satisfying the oxygen requirements of the muscles and the removal of anaerobic metabolites.\" This \"Differentiation of the vesicular reaction providing effective redistribution of blood flow\" (Inference: \"vesicular\" is probably meant as \"vascular\") develops in the competition period \"as a result of the extensive aerobic loading executed during the preparatory period.\" In 25 km road cycling, improved performance came with a fall in VO2 max in the competition period while \"peak blood flow to the lower extremities increases,\" and cardiac output also fell (Stepochkin et al 1970). (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image.) \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\": while VO2 max falls somewhat in the competition period, \"OBLA can rise\" (Zatsiorsky et al 1974; Sirenko 1979; Nurmekivan 1974). In qualified skaters the mean change in VO2 max in the competition period is \"5-10%,\" while local blood flow rises \"much more significantly (50-250%)\" (Mellenberg 1981). The authors conclude that training effectiveness \"does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport,\" and that, with the muscles' strength and oxidative qualities, \"the redistribution of bloodflow and improved local vesicular reaction are important conditions for developing local muscle endurance.\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250, verified against the page image.) Because these conditions lessen dependence on VO2 max, the assumed \"antagonism in endurance sport between the development of the aerobic and anaerobic mechanisms must be questioned (Mellenberg & Khvan, 1982)\" (PDF pp. 260-261 / printed pp. 249-250).\nclaim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths.md:29:threshold (or turnpoint) or OBLA (Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation)\" (printed 78). (PDF\nclaim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths.md:44:irrelevant to training; the blood lactate threshold (OBLA) remains a legitimate, useful","stderr":"