:52.071Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max.md:26:Inference: the passages may describe different aims (a VO2 max reserve before the season; muscle-level economy as the main target), but the text does not reconcile them. Also unclear on the same page: \"Anaerobic work \nclaim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains.md:16:**What it is** — \"Recently, haemodynamic factors have been shown to play an important role in the functional specialisation of the body during endurance work\" (nine citations). In italics: \"The redistribution of blood\nclaim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains.md:22:**When NOT to apply** — A sentence on the same page is unclear: \"blood lactate concentration can increase by some 6% as a result of training, while the VO2 max level increases by only 7% (Williams et al, 1967)\" (PDF p\nclaim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths.md:22:below the lactate threshold, so current theory attributes soreness to transient muscle\nclaim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths.md:29:threshold (or turnpoint) or OBLA (Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation)\" (printed 78). (PDF\nclaim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths.md:44:irrelevant to training; the blood lactate threshold (OBLA) remains a legitimate, useful\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths.md\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--macs-mics-integrated-theory-of-electrostimulation.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"635d75fb-7948-421f-9cd2-05865cb049eb","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
