{"parentUuid":"4575fbf2-7265-42a8-a4f1-ecf75845fef3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MqF5GUUASQX8g1BFCxMQDh","type":"tool_result","content":"# The Authors Locate Endurance in the Muscles' Oxidative and Strength Qualities Rather Than in VO2 Max, but Give Conflicting Advice on Glycolytic Work\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Oxidative Capacity and Muscular Endurance\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image), the authors cite research that endurance gains are \"associated more with enhancing the ability of the muscles to utilise a higher percentage of the oxygen already in the blood than with increasing the amount of oxygen in the bloodstream\" (Ekblom 1969; Rowell 1971; Saltin 1974). \"A rise in intramuscular energy potential, the power of the oxidative processes and the contractile (strength) qualities of the muscles decrease the rate of glycolysis\" (four citations), and lactate falls \"in the skeletal muscles and not in the liver and myocardium, as has always been believed\" (Jorfeldt 1970; Knutten 1971; Gollnick & Hermansen 1973). Their italicised conclusion: \"the development of endurance is associated with functional specialisation of the skeletal muscles, particularly the enhancement of their strength and oxidative qualities, rather than improvement of cardiorespiratory ability.\" So the emphasis should be \"to decrease the proportion of glycolysis in supplying the energy for work and improve the ability of the muscles to oxidise lactate during work, and not simply to improve accommodation to high concentrations of blood lactate,\" aiming \"chiefly at eliminating the disparity between the anaerobic and aerobic abilities of the muscles.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The mechanism the authors give: stronger, more oxidative muscle relies less on glycolysis, makes less lactate and oxidises more of it in the muscle itself. They support it with citations, not with data shown in the text.\n\n**When to apply** — When planning endurance or repeat-effort conditioning: include strength work and work that develops the working muscles' oxidative capacity, not only central (heart-lung) work. Eric's baseline: \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 124). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning is a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice [**Explicit**, line 116], though \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" [**Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**, line 117]. Inference: the authors' \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" line supports the first; their own p.249 and p.253 statements (below) support the off-season glycolytic work.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Why `contested`: the authors' own pages pull in opposite directions.\n- Raise VO2 max, or not: \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max, and in particular, to create some reserve of aerobic power by the end of the preparatory period\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249), against \"effectiveness of training does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\n- Glycolytic work, more or less: \"it is advisable to execute a specific part of the training load with a high blood lactate concentration\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249) and, for skaters, \"it [is] necessary to have excellent movement technique and a high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity\" (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253), against \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249).\n\nInference: the passages may describe different aims (a VO2 max reserve before the season; muscle-level economy as the main target), but the text does not reconcile them. Also unclear on the same page: \"Anaerobic work is approximately 50% more effective than aerobic work in raising this lactate threshold, therefore it is to the athlete's advantage to execute prolonged, intense work without the preliminary accumulation of lactate and include anaerobic energy production (Christensen & Hogberg, 1950)\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249); the \"therefore\" does not follow clearly from the 50% figure, and no measure of \"effective\" is given. The sources are 1950-1975 and are not described.\n\n**Example** — The book gives no training example in this passage. Inference: a winger's conditioning block that adds heavy lower-body strength work and longer threshold-paced intervals, rather than only more aerobic volume, follows the authors' main line.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]], [[claim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths]] (Ch1.15, lactate is not a toxin; agreement on a muscle-centred view of lactate), [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]] (the p.253 glycolytic statement), [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]].","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"07cd3ed5-a560-4630-938b-69591905c3c2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:48:54.689Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# The Authors Locate Endurance in the Muscles' Oxidative and Strength Qualities Rather Than in VO2 Max, but Give Conflicting Advice on Glycolytic Work\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Oxidative Capacity and Muscular Endurance\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image), the authors cite research that endurance gains are \"associated more with enhancing the ability of the muscles to utilise a higher percentage of the oxygen already in the blood than with increasing the amount of oxygen in the bloodstream\" (Ekblom 1969; Rowell 1971; Saltin 1974). \"A rise in intramuscular energy potential, the power of the oxidative processes and the contractile (strength) qualities of the muscles decrease the rate of glycolysis\" (four citations), and lactate falls \"in the skeletal muscles and not in the liver and myocardium, as has always been believed\" (Jorfeldt 1970; Knutten 1971; Gollnick & Hermansen 1973). Their italicised conclusion: \"the development of endurance is associated with functional specialisation of the skeletal muscles, particularly the enhancement of their strength and oxidative qualities, rather than improvement of cardiorespiratory ability.\" So the emphasis should be \"to decrease the proportion of glycolysis in supplying the energy for work and improve the ability of the muscles to oxidise lactate during work, and not simply to improve accommodation to high concentrations of blood lactate,\" aiming \"chiefly at eliminating the disparity between the anaerobic and aerobic abilities of the muscles.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The mechanism the authors give: stronger, more oxidative muscle relies less on glycolysis, makes less lactate and oxidises more of it in the muscle itself. They support it with citations, not with data shown in the text.\n\n**When to apply** — When planning endurance or repeat-effort conditioning: include strength work and work that develops the working muscles' oxidative capacity, not only central (heart-lung) work. Eric's baseline: \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 124). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning is a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice [**Explicit**, line 116], though \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" [**Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**, line 117]. Inference: the authors' \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" line supports the first; their own p.249 and p.253 statements (below) support the off-season glycolytic work.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Why `contested`: the authors' own pages pull in opposite directions.\n- Raise VO2 max, or not: \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max, and in particular, to create some reserve of aerobic power by the end of the preparatory period\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249), against \"effectiveness of training does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\n- Glycolytic work, more or less: \"it is advisable to execute a specific part of the training load with a high blood lactate concentration\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249) and, for skaters, \"it [is] necessary to have excellent movement technique and a high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity\" (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253), against \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249).\n\nInference: the passages may describe different aims (a VO2 max reserve before the season; muscle-level economy as the main target), but the text does not reconcile them. Also unclear on the same page: \"Anaerobic work is approximately 50% more effective than aerobic work in raising this lactate threshold, therefore it is to the athlete's advantage to execute prolonged, intense work without the preliminary accumulation of lactate and include anaerobic energy production (Christensen & Hogberg, 1950)\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249); the \"therefore\" does not follow clearly from the 50% figure, and no measure of \"effective\" is given. The sources are 1950-1975 and are not described.\n\n**Example** — The book gives no training example in this passage. Inference: a winger's conditioning block that adds heavy lower-body strength work and longer threshold-paced intervals, rather than only more aerobic volume, follows the authors' main line.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]], [[claim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths]] (Ch1.15, lactate is not a toxin; agreement on a muscle-centred view of lactate), [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]] (the p.253 glycolytic statement), [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4575fbf2-7265-42a8-a4f1-ecf75845fef3","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
