{"parentUuid":"7512bd6c-4786-4765-90e7-f197d4da6b5a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MB7XuEfpBFaJRhJXKrAMRn","type":"tool_result","content":"Following these requirements can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines  which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22). However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which  fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria. In example illustrated, the partner seated on the athlete's  shoulders simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes, while the partner pulling the back leg  offers resistance intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors. While this exercise may be appropriate if  applied carefully, incorrect positioning or imposition of resistance can reduce its effectiveness markedly. Flgirt 4.22 A special strength exercise intended to simulate the flexion-extension action of the hip during sports activities, where the  Kccntuated regions o f force production can be incorrectly resisted and posture can be inappropriately held (Siff & Verkhoshansky,  1999). It is possible to develop force at the required joint angles with isometric exercises, because they can have a  particular value in this respect (see 4.2.4). With isometrics or quasi-isometrics over a given range of movement  one can locally influence certain muscle groups by paying particular attention to the accentuated regions of the  movements where they arc involved. Therefore, the selection of these exercises should be based on the joint angle  it which maximal torque is developed in the special exercise. 4.3.3 The Dynam ics of the Ef","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d8e69d56-ffa3-41c7-adfd-af8e9584e9e3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:51:05.842Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"Following these requirements can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines  which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22). However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which  fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria. In example illustrated, the partner seated on the athlete's  shoulders simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes, while the partner pulling the back leg  offers resistance intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors. While this exercise may be appropriate if  applied carefully, incorrect positioning or imposition of resistance can reduce its effectiveness markedly. Flgirt 4.22 A special strength exercise intended to simulate the flexion-extension action of the hip during sports activities, where the  Kccntuated regions o f force production can be incorrectly resisted and posture can be inappropriately held (Siff & Verkhoshansky,  1999). It is possible to develop force at the required joint angles with isometric exercises, because they can have a  particular value in this respect (see 4.2.4). With isometrics or quasi-isometrics over a given range of movement  one can locally influence certain muscle groups by paying particular attention to the accentuated regions of the  movements where they arc involved. Therefore, the selection of these exercises should be based on the joint angle  it which maximal torque is developed in the special exercise. 4.3.3 The Dynam ics of the Ef","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7512bd6c-4786-4765-90e7-f197d4da6b5a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
