{"parentUuid":"c735070e-c123-44e0-b49c-1a3c40480454","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVUiStsEPv3R8u5mW9L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the power entry. I'm keeping the slug so the RFD entry's link still resolves, and keeping `proven` scoped only to the instantaneous identity."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":691,"cache_read_input_tokens":116543,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":691,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffVUg7kycMe11qiPBvwa","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"42678879-9fcc-47eb-8928-8044a442854c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:51:37.119Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"42678879-9fcc-47eb-8928-8044a442854c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVUiStsEPv3R8u5mW9L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019A4JxM5rBZpaUgjoQt5CNX","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md'; s=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\nreps=[\n(\"# P = F x V Holds Only When Force Is Constant; Explosive Actions Need the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\",\n \"# The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\"),\n(\"**What it is** — Scope of the `proven` rating: only the mathematical point that P = dW/dt reduces to P = F.V when force is constant. The claim that \\\"many strength training texts\\\" misuse it, and the advice on force plates and video, are the authors' judgment. The authors define power as the instantaneous rate of doing work, P = dW/dt,\",\n \"**What it is** — Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works). The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise and is not part of that scope; the claim that \\\"many strength training texts\\\" misuse the formula, and the advice on force plates and video, are the authors' judgment. The authors define power as P = dW/dt, \\\"The rate of doing work at any instant,\\\"\"),\n(\"**Why it works** — This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case of P = dW/dt with constant force, so using it with a time-varying force gives a wrong instantaneous picture. Scope of `proven`: the mathematical relation only (P = dW/dt reduces to F.V under constant force). The practical judgment\",\n \"**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: with W = ∫F(t).ds, \\\"if F is constant over the interval concerned,\\\" power reduces to \\\"the familiar basic expression for power,\\\" P = F.V; \\\"If the force is not constant over the time period being analysed, then we have to rely on a more precise form such as equation (4.1)\\\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246). Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force. The authors state that P = F.V needs constant force; constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval (then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity). Inference: because dW = F·ds, the rate of work is dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant, whatever the force history, so the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact. What fails for a time-varying force is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity: the average of a product is not in general the product of the averages, and peak force and peak velocity need not occur at the same instant. That is the misuse the authors' warning (\\\"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\\\") actually targets. The practical judgment\"),\n(\"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\",\n \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:60]; s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nEOF\nsed -n 14,20p claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md","description":"Apply physics fix to power entry"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":691,"cache_read_input_tokens":116543,"output_tokens":2434,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1090},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":691},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2434,"cache_read_input_tokens":116543,"cache_creation_input_tokens":691,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":691,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_019A4JxM5rBZpaUgjoQt5CNX":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md'; s=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\nreps=[\n(\"# P = F x V Holds Only When Force Is Constant; Explosive Actions Need the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\",\n \"# The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\"),\n(\"**What it is** — Scope of the `proven` rating: only the mathematical point that P = dW/dt reduces to P = F.V when force is constant. The claim that \\\"many strength training texts\\\" misuse it, and the advice on force plates and video, are the authors' judgment. The authors define power as the instantaneous rate of doing work, P = dW/dt,\",\n \"**What it is** — Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works). The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise and is not part of that scope; the claim that \\\"many strength training texts\\\" misuse the formula, and the advice on force plates and video, are the authors' judgment. The authors define power as P = dW/dt, \\\"The rate of doing work at any instant,\\\"\"),\n(\"**Why it works** — This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case of P = dW/dt with constant force, so using it with a time-varying force gives a wrong instantaneous picture. Scope of `proven`: the mathematical relation only (P = dW/dt reduces to F.V under constant force). The practical judgment\",\n \"**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: with W = ∫F(t).ds, \\\"if F is constant over the interval concerned,\\\" power reduces to \\\"the familiar basic expression for power,\\\" P = F.V; \\\"If the force is not constant over the time period being analysed, then we have to rely on a more precise form such as equation (4.1)\\\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246). Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force. The authors state that P = F.V needs constant force; constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval (then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity). Inference: because dW = F·ds, the rate of work is dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant, whatever the force history, so the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact. What fails for a time-varying force is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity: the average of a product is not in general the product of the averages, and peak force and peak velocity need not occur at the same instant. That is the misuse the authors' warning (\\\"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\\\") actually targets. The practical judgment\"),\n(\"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\",\n \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:60]; s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nEOF\nsed -n 14,20p claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md","description":"Apply physics fix to power entry"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffVUg7kycMe11qiPBvwa","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9dcf3916-da98-4dc4-89b6-b248fe333af5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:51:45.912Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"9dcf3916-da98-4dc4-89b6-b248fe333af5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019A4JxM5rBZpaUgjoQt5CNX","type":"tool_result","content":"# The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\n\n**What it is** — Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works). The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise and is not part of that scope; the claim that \"many strength training texts\" misuse the formula, and the advice on force plates and video, are the authors' judgment. The authors define power as P = dW/dt, \"The rate of doing work at any instant,\" with work W = ∫F(t).ds, and note that \"if F is constant over the interval concerned\" this reduces to \"the familiar basic expression for power\": P = F.V, where V is the velocity of the object (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246; the equations are images, verified against the page image). In italics: \"this basic definition is applied without qualification in many strength training texts and can lead to serious misunderstanding of training theory and methods.\" When force is not constant, one must use the general form (equation 4.1) and look at how force and velocity vary over time, \"most conveniently\" from F-V, F-t and V-t graphs. \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone.\" In plyometric actions \"the force changes very rapidly as a complicated transient spike function and cannot be regarded as a constant throughout the rebound phase,\" so \"it is better to rely on a force plate or high speed video methods to analyse the movement than to attempt to apply elementary biomechanical calculations that will inevitably produce grave errors.\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: with W = ∫F(t).ds, \"if F is constant over the interval concerned,\" power reduces to \"the familiar basic expression for power,\" P = F.V; \"If the force is not constant over the time period being analysed, then we have to rely on a more precise form such as equation (4.1)\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246). Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force. The authors state that P = F.V needs constant force; constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval (then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity). Inference: because dW = F·ds, the rate of work is dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant, whatever the force history, so the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact. What fails for a time-varying force is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity: the average of a product is not in general the product of the averages, and peak force and peak velocity need not occur at the same instant. That is the misuse the authors' warning (\"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\") actually targets. The practical judgment that \"many strength training texts\" misuse it is the authors' assessment, with no citations.\n\n**When to apply** — When reporting or comparing power or explosive strength: use instantaneous force-time and velocity-time data rather than one average force times one average velocity, and read the shape of the force-time curve, not just its peak or mean. For plyometric and rebound actions, measure on a force plate or with high-speed video. Eric's baseline: the force-plate reporting framework adds \"'fluffy' metrics (peak power, average power, RFD) only with a specific, defensible rationale.\" Tagged **Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 79). Inference: the authors' point gives one reason for that caution: single power numbers hide how force changed over the action.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d91fa012-a380-41e0-8d32-9d749756d280","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:51:46.358Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\n\n**What it is** — Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works). The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise and is not part of that scope; the claim that \"many strength training texts\" misuse the formula, and the advice on force plates and video, are the authors' judgment. The authors define power as P = dW/dt, \"The rate of doing work at any instant,\" with work W = ∫F(t).ds, and note that \"if F is constant over the interval concerned\" this reduces to \"the familiar basic expression for power\": P = F.V, where V is the velocity of the object (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246; the equations are images, verified against the page image). In italics: \"this basic definition is applied without qualification in many strength training texts and can lead to serious misunderstanding of training theory and methods.\" When force is not constant, one must use the general form (equation 4.1) and look at how force and velocity vary over time, \"most conveniently\" from F-V, F-t and V-t graphs. \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone.\" In plyometric actions \"the force changes very rapidly as a complicated transient spike function and cannot be regarded as a constant throughout the rebound phase,\" so \"it is better to rely on a force plate or high speed video methods to analyse the movement than to attempt to apply elementary biomechanical calculations that will inevitably produce grave errors.\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: with W = ∫F(t).ds, \"if F is constant over the interval concerned,\" power reduces to \"the familiar basic expression for power,\" P = F.V; \"If the force is not constant over the time period being analysed, then we have to rely on a more precise form such as equation (4.1)\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246). Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force. The authors state that P = F.V needs constant force; constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval (then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity). Inference: because dW = F·ds, the rate of work is dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant, whatever the force history, so the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact. What fails for a time-varying force is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity: the average of a product is not in general the product of the averages, and peak force and peak velocity need not occur at the same instant. That is the misuse the authors' warning (\"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\") actually targets. The practical judgment that \"many strength training texts\" misuse it is the authors' assessment, with no citations.\n\n**When to apply** — When reporting or comparing power or explosive strength: use instantaneous force-time and velocity-time data rather than one average force times one average velocity, and read the shape of the force-time curve, not just its peak or mean. For plyometric and rebound actions, measure on a force plate or with high-speed video. Eric's baseline: the force-plate reporting framework adds \"'fluffy' metrics (peak power, average power, RFD) only with a specific, defensible rationale.\" Tagged **Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 79). Inference: the authors' point gives one reason for that caution: single power numbers hide how force changed over the action.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9dcf3916-da98-4dc4-89b6-b248fe333af5","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
