{"parentUuid":"464b15ca-0c7d-4ed9-9836-14be04bb929f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVWCG1364hZvg8UxsWU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The \"constant force is needed only when...\" clause is my physics and sits outside the Inference label. Moving it inside."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2536,"cache_read_input_tokens":118378,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2536,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffVW9tt3V4qw3gU9HdGW","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9eeee71b-2165-4c61-9928-25f1db44ea22","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:51:50.460Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"9eeee71b-2165-4c61-9928-25f1db44ea22","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVWCG1364hZvg8UxsWU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VAMCZqbKnqRTehXgzuBtYV","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md'; s=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\na=\"which holds instant by instant for any force. The authors state that P = F.V needs constant force; constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval (then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity). Inference: because dW = F·ds, the rate of work is dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant, whatever the force history, so the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact. What fails\"\nb=\"which holds instant by instant for any force; the authors' constant-force condition is not part of it. Inference: because dW = F·ds, the rate of work is dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant, whatever the force history, so the instantaneous product F(t)·v(t) is exact. Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval: then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity. What fails\"\nassert s.count(a)==1; s=s.replace(a,b); open(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nEOF","description":"Move physics clause under Inference label"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2536,"cache_read_input_tokens":118378,"output_tokens":810,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":257},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_inpu