{"parentUuid":"ad41ab48-9985-4e0f-9460-d4fc985002b8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVXVz99Xrxr1LALEcaM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qp1drhjXnbvEWbiqGbyidD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; sed -n 16,26p heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md; grep -n \"4.22\" heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | cut -c1-120","description":"Read the edited accentuated-angle passage"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1286,"cache_read_input_tokens":121763,"output_tokens":258,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":52},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1286},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":258,"cache_read_input_tokens":121763,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1286,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1286,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Qp1drhjXnbvEWbiqGbyidD":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; sed -n 16,26p heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md; grep -n \"4.22\" heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | cut -c1-120","description":"Read the edited accentuated-angle passage"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffVXTWJapWTzAo45TBSj","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"52ef62c7-c895-4080-a10c-4372a65eaf33","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:52:07.259Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"52ef62c7-c895-4080-a10c-4372a65eaf33","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qp1drhjXnbvEWbiqGbyidD","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is** — \"Muscular effort changes during the course of every movement and maximum force is developed at the most appropriate instant for a given action.\" In ballistic movements that instant is \"the beginning part of the working amplitude\"; in movements of mixed regimes it is \"the instant of switching from one regime to another\" (Verkhoshansky 1977). So \"the working amplitude always has an accentuated region where the maximum dynamic force occurs close to a specific joint angle,\" and the criterion of correspondence \"includes the need to produce the required force at a specific joint angle\" (PDF p. 255 / printed p. 244). For the hip-flexor example of 4.3.1, the accentuated region is near the beginning of the leg's swing, shown by Fig 4.21 (verified against the page image: torque rises to a peak of about 700 N.m within roughly the first 0.02-0.03 s, falls below about 200 N.m by about 0.06-0.08 s, and stays low to 0.160 s). Athletes \"who run with loads or use hip flexor machines regularly\" thus fail twice: they miss the full amplitude and \"lose the ability to train the muscles to generate the necessary force at the appropriate hip joint angle\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245). The exercise \"should not only reproduce the full amplitude of the movement but also the specific direction of resistance to the pull of the muscles.\" Isometrics or quasi-isometrics can target the required angles (see 4.2.4), and \"the selection of these exercises should be based on the joint angle at which maximal torque is developed in the special exercise\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245).\n\nThe authors say that following these requirements \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22),\" but add: \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245). Fig 4.22 (verified against the page image: one partner on the athlete's shoulders, another pulling the athlete's back leg, the athlete against wall bars) is a partner exercise in which, per the text, the partner on the shoulders \"simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes\" and the partner on the back leg offers resistance \"intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors.\" Its caption says the accentuated regions \"can be incorrectly resisted and posture can be inappropriately held\"; the text says it \"may be appropriate if applied carefully,\" but incorrect positioning \"can reduce its effectiveness markedly\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is that the force peak of a sport action is tied to a joint angle, so training that peaks elsewhere in the range does not prepare that angle. They give no physiological mechanism here. Inference: this is the same angle-specific transfer question treated in Ch2 and 4.2.4 (see Related), applied to exercise choice.\n\n**When to apply** — Before choosing a special strength or isometric exercise: identify where in the sport action peak force occurs (early in a ballistic action, or at the switch between regimes), then set the starting position, range and resistance direction so the exercise peaks at that angle. Use isometric or quasi-isometric holds at that angle when the dynamic exercise cannot be positioned correctly. Eric's baseline: the isometric seated calf raise is run on ForceDecks at a \"90-degree ankle/knee position.\" Tagged **Inferred (protocol)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 78). Inference: that is a fixed-angle test, not a training choice; by this criterion, an isometric training angle would be chosen from where the sport action peaks, which may not be 90 degrees.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Source mismatch on Fig 4.21: the running text calls it \"the graph of hip flexion torque in the air,\" while the caption reads \"Change in the moment of the force during repetitive flexion-extension of the leg during the take-off for the triple jump\" (PDF p. 255 / printed p. 244). Inference: the take-off caption does not obviously describe a swing-leg hip flexion torque, so use the figure as an illustration of an early peak, not as hip-flexor data. The book's other sections qualify strict angle matching: isometric gains spread up to about 15 degrees either side of the training angle and further when the muscle trains lengthened (4.2.4), and Ch2 reports that training at a shortened angle transfers less across the range. Inference: training only at the accentuated angle may give less carryover to the rest of the range, so the criterion fits a supplementary exercise better than a whole program.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: sprinters' hip flexor work, where peak torque falls near the start of the leg's forward swing, so standing hip-flexor machines and loaded running miss it (PDF pp. 255-256 / printed pp. 244-245).\n18:The authors say that following these requirements \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pull","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e126d594-5bcb-4059-b875-d671b1b40c70","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:52:07.536Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**What it is** — \"Muscular effort changes during the course of every movement and maximum force is developed at the most appropriate instant for a given action.\" In ballistic movements that instant is \"the beginning part of the working amplitude\"; in movements of mixed regimes it is \"the instant of switching from one regime to another\" (Verkhoshansky 1977). So \"the working amplitude always has an accentuated region where the maximum dynamic force occurs close to a specific joint angle,\" and the criterion of correspondence \"includes the need to produce the required force at a specific joint angle\" (PDF p. 255 / printed p. 244). For the hip-flexor example of 4.3.1, the accentuated region is near the beginning of the leg's swing, shown by Fig 4.21 (verified against the page image: torque rises to a peak of about 700 N.m within roughly the first 0.02-0.03 s, falls below about 200 N.m by about 0.06-0.08 s, and stays low to 0.160 s). Athletes \"who run with loads or use hip flexor machines regularly\" thus fail twice: they miss the full amplitude and \"lose the ability to train the muscles to generate the necessary force at the appropriate hip joint angle\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245). The exercise \"should not only reproduce the full amplitude of the movement but also the specific direction of resistance to the pull of the muscles.\" Isometrics or quasi-isometrics can target the required angles (see 4.2.4), and \"the selection of these exercises should be based on the joint angle at which maximal torque is developed in the special exercise\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245).\n\nThe authors say that following these requirements \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22),\" but add: \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245). Fig 4.22 (verified against the page image: one partner on the athlete's shoulders, another pulling the athlete's back leg, the athlete against wall bars) is a partner exercise in which, per the text, the partner on the shoulders \"simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes\" and the partner on the back leg offers resistance \"intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors.\" Its caption says the accentuated regions \"can be incorrectly resisted and posture can be inappropriately held\"; the text says it \"may be appropriate if applied carefully,\" but incorrect positioning \"can reduce its effectiveness markedly\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is that the force peak of a sport action is tied to a joint angle, so training that peaks elsewhere in the range does not prepare that angle. They give no physiological mechanism here. Inference: this is the same angle-specific transfer question treated in Ch2 and 4.2.4 (see Related), applied to exercise choice.\n\n**When to apply** — Before choosing a special strength or isometric exercise: identify where in the sport action peak force occurs (early in a ballistic action, or at the switch between regimes), then set the starting position, range and resistance direction so the exercise peaks at that angle. Use isometric or quasi-isometric holds at that angle when the dynamic exercise cannot be positioned correctly. Eric's baseline: the isometric seated calf raise is run on ForceDecks at a \"90-degree ankle/knee position.\" Tagged **Inferred (protocol)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 78). Inference: that is a fixed-angle test, not a training choice; by this criterion, an isometric training angle would be chosen from where the sport action peaks, which may not be 90 degrees.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Source mismatch on Fig 4.21: the running text calls it \"the graph of hip flexion torque in the air,\" while the caption reads \"Change in the moment of the force during repetitive flexion-extension of the leg during the take-off for the triple jump\" (PDF p. 255 / printed p. 244). Inference: the take-off caption does not obviously describe a swing-leg hip flexion torque, so use the figure as an illustration of an early peak, not as hip-flexor data. The book's other sections qualify strict angle matching: isometric gains spread up to about 15 degrees either side of the training angle and further when the muscle trains lengthened (4.2.4), and Ch2 reports that training at a shortened angle transfers less across the range. Inference: training only at the accentuated angle may give less carryover to the rest of the range, so the criterion fits a supplementary exercise better than a whole program.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: sprinters' hip flexor work, where peak torque falls near the start of the leg's forward swing, so standing hip-flexor machines and loaded running miss it (PDF pp. 255-256 / printed pp. 244-245).\n18:The authors say that following these requirements \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pull","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"52ef62c7-c895-4080-a10c-4372a65eaf33","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
