{"parentUuid":"2c5d8644-ed17-41ce-8528-e77b2674244a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVXkv4sLon6bV7w2LGa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the parachute, boxer, high jumper, and skater fixes."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1949,"cache_read_input_tokens":123049,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1949,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffVXiYCQfnXAUjfruJAv","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c946483b-2c78-4387-bb28-85a69a079f26","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:52:09.406Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"c946483b-2c78-4387-bb28-85a69a079f26","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVXkv4sLon6bV7w2LGa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RAasYQDASFDEXSLZaWAiu1","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; python3 - <<'EOF'\ndef fix(p,a,b):\n    s=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read(); assert s.count(a)==1,(p,a[:50]); open(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s.replace(a,b))\nfix('heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md',\n \"**Why it works** — The physics the authors give (drag rising with the square of velocity) explains why parachute size and running speed together set the load: drag in fluid flow scaling with velocity squared is standard fluid mechanics. Inference: that physical relation is the `proven` core here;\",\n \"**Why it works** — The authors give the physics: the parachutes' aerodynamic resistance \\\"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity),\\\" which \\\"allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably\\\" (PDF p. 255 / printed p. 244); that is why parachute size and running speed together set the load. Inference: velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`;\")\nfix('claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md',\n \"if vertical loading is the concern (heavy athletes, athletes returning from lower-limb injury), the authors' argument favours the parachute; if preserving flight-phase and landing mechanics is the concern (hurdlers, jumpers), it counts against it.\",\n \"if vertical loading is the concern, the authors' argument favours the parachute (Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population); if preserving flight-phase and landing mechanics is the concern (the book names hurdlers and jumpers), it counts against it.\")\nfix('heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md',\n \"single maximal actions against a fixed implement call for heavier loads, few repetitions and full recovery.\",\n \"single fast actions against a set resistance call for heavier loads and few repetitions (Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest).\")\nfix('case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md',\n \"give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position and less to plantarflexor power.\",\n \"give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position. Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction.\")\nfix('case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md',\n \"Inference: the skaters' jump in \\\"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\\\" (2.7% to 36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point.\",\n \"Inference: the rise of \\\"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\\\" to the first factor at Master of Sport level (36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point; the Class 1 value for the same factor (2.7) is unclear, so the size of the change should not be quoted.\")\nEOF\nsed -n '/When NOT to apply/p' case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md","description":"Apply five straightforward fixes"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1949,"cache_read_input_tokens":123049,"output_tokens":1275,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":34},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1949},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1275,"cache_read_input_tokens":123049,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1949,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1949,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01RAasYQDASFDEXSLZaWAiu1":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; python3 - <<'EOF'\ndef fix(p,a,b):\n    s=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read(); assert s.count(a)==1,(p,a[:50]); open(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s.replace(a,b))\nfix('heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md',\n \"**Why it works** — The physics the authors give (drag rising with the square of velocity) explains why parachute size and running speed together set the load: drag in fluid flow scaling with velocity squared is standard fluid mechanics. Inference: that physical relation is the `proven` core here;\",\n \"**Why it works** — The authors give the physics: the parachutes' aerodynamic resistance \\\"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity),\\\" which \\\"allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably\\\" (PDF p. 255 / printed p. 244); that is why parachute size and running speed together set the load. Inference: velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`;\")\nfix('claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md',\n \"if vertical loading is the concern (heavy athletes, athletes returning from lower-limb injury), the authors' argument favours the parachute; if preserving flight-phase and landing mechanics is the concern (hurdlers, jumpers), it counts against it.\",\n \"if vertical loading is the concern, the authors' argument favours the parachute (Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population); if preserving flight-phase and landing mechanics is the concern (the book names hurdlers and jumpers), it counts against it.\")\nfix('heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md',\n \"single maximal actions against a fixed implement call for heavier loads, few repetitions and full recovery.\",\n \"single fast actions against a set resistance call for heavier loads and few repetitions (Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest).\")\nfix('case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md',\n \"give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position and less to plantarflexor power.\",\n \"give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position. Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction.\")\nfix('case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md',\n \"Inference: the skaters' jump in \\\"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\\\" (2.7% to 36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point.\",\n \"Inference: the rise of \\\"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\\\" to the first factor at Master of Sport level (36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point; the Class 1 value for the same factor (2.7) is unclear, so the size of the change should not be quoted.\")\nEOF\nsed -n '/When NOT to apply/p' case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md","description":"Apply five straightforward fixes"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffVXiYCQfnXAUjfruJAv","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"90b20119-a745-404f-8b06-19ad4fc3c9d0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:52:17.330Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"90b20119-a745-404f-8b06-19ad4fc3c9d0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2a81cff2-a7a8-4e62-bcc6-0c023068f2b2","agentId":"a3aafe568bae89c86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RAasYQDASFDEXSLZaWAiu1","type":"tool_result","content":"**When NOT to apply** — The sample is \"a large sample\" with no number, method or test battery given. The columns do not sum to 100 (about 88% and 89%), and the remainder is not explained. Factor labels differ between columns, so the comparison is interpretive. The conclusion that a \"high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity\" is necessary conflicts with the authors' earlier advice in 4.4 to \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249); see [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]].","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f41e5f96-b597-4a2c-b2be-e71976d187ef","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:52:17.743Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**When NOT to apply** — The sample is \"a large sample\" with no number, method or test battery given. The columns do not sum to 100 (about 88% and 89%), and the remainder is not explained. Factor labels differ between columns, so the comparison is interpretive. The conclusion that a \"high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity\" is necessary conflicts with the authors' earlier advice in 4.4 to \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249); see [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"90b20119-a745-404f-8b06-19ad4fc3c9d0","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
